window.QUIZ_ANSWERS={"Q1-01": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>1. Q1-01 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A warehouse robot can move horizontally and vertically. In one operating mode it performs the two movements sequentially; in another it performs them simultaneously. For a destination requiring 6 and 8 seconds on the two axes, which statements correctly connect the distance measure to the task?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Sequential travel takes 14 seconds and is represented by L1 on these time coordinates; C. Simultaneous travel takes 8 seconds and is represented by L-infinity on these time coordinates; D. Switching movement rules can change which station is nearest in operational travel time</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> L1 adds sequential requirements; L-infinity takes the longest concurrent requirement. Geometry must reflect the operational meaning of the coordinates. Euclidean distance would be appropriate for unrestricted straight-line distance, not automatically for either stated timing rule. Changing the metric can reorder candidate stations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-02": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>2. Q1-02 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which activities belong to model selection and must avoid using the final test outcomes?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Choosing k for k-NN; C. Choosing an SVM kernel; D. Choosing a classification threshold</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Tuning neighbors, kernels, and thresholds uses outcome information to choose a procedure. Final testing follows that selection and should not trigger more tuning.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-03": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>3. Q1-03 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A quality team tunes a soft-margin SVM using the stated objective total classification-loss penalty + lambda times squared coefficient norm. It raises lambda, and training fit becomes worse. Which interpretations are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Validation is needed to judge whether the tradeoff improves generalization; D. The change places more emphasis on the coefficient penalty</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Lambda multiplies the coefficient-norm penalty here, so increasing it favors a more regularized, wider-margin tradeoff rather than maximizing training fit. Held-out performance determines whether that tradeoff helps. Training loss need not decrease. This question requires reading the stated penalty convention, not deriving the SVM optimization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-04": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>4. Q1-04 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A repair-time tree sends a new machine into a leaf containing training repair times 10, 14, and 18 hours. The manager claims the tree must predict exactly 14 hours and guarantee that the next repair will take 14 hours. What is the best correction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. An ordinary regression leaf predicts 14 hours, but the prediction is not a guarantee for an individual repair</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The ordinary constant-response regression leaf uses the mean, (10+14+18)/3=14. That is an estimate for cases reaching the leaf, not certainty about a new outcome. Voting belongs to classification, and fitting a full regression in every leaf is not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q1-05": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>5. Q1-05 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A city needs different daytime and nighttime patrol locations. Which changes are analytically reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Check patrol coverage and budgets for each period; C. Cluster nighttime incidents separately; D. Cluster daytime incidents separately</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Different time regimes can have different spatial distributions. Separate clusterings can align patrol placement with when incidents occur; sample size alone does not make pooling appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-06": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>6. Q1-06 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An engineer wants an alarm for a sustained increase in axle temperature rather than a future price forecast. Which lecture method directly serves this purpose?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. CUSUM</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> CUSUM accumulates evidence of a mean shift over ordered observations. The other methods address dimension reduction, grouping, or counts.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q1-07": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>7. Q1-07 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A lender needs a probability that a borrower defaults and then a yes/no intervention rule. Historical default outcomes are coded 1 and 0; borrower attributes are available before lending. Which statements are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Logistic regression directly models a binary-event probability from attributes; C. The intervention threshold should reflect the costs of missed defaults and unnecessary interventions; D. A threshold can turn the estimated probability into a class or intervention decision</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Logistic regression targets probability; choosing a threshold creates a decision layer. A raw SVM score is not automatically a probability. A default 50% threshold is not automatically best when the consequences differ. The probability model and the action rule answer related but different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a></p>"}, "Q1-08": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>8. Q1-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about single exponential smoothing are correct for 0&lt;alpha&lt;1?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Recent observations receive larger weights than distant ones; C. Older observations remain represented in the previous level; D. A larger alpha follows new observations more quickly</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The recurrence compresses past information with geometrically declining weights. Single smoothing has no explicit trend or seasonal component.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-09": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>9. Q1-09 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A plant compares a linear SVM with a classification tree. The SVM uses the rule x1&gt;5, independent of x2. The tree also splits on x2 and reduces training errors from 12 to 11 by isolating one training observation. No held-out results are available. Which conclusions follow?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The isolated observation raises an overfitting concern; D. x2 does not affect predictions under the stated SVM rule</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The stated boundary depends only on x1. The tiny leaf may fit an idiosyncratic training case; fewer training errors and more predictors do not establish predictive superiority. The evidence warrants a validation comparison, not a universal claim that either model always wins.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q1-10": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>10. Q1-10 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A diagram has x1 on the horizontal axis and x2 on the vertical axis. A classifier predicts rejection to the right of the vertical line x1=4. A rejected observation moves upward without changing x1. Which interpretation is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It remains rejected under this rule, although a different fitted model could still use x2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Crossing this boundary requires changing the horizontal coordinate x1. Upward movement leaves the class unchanged. Irrelevance is specific to this fitted rule; a different boundary or a tree can use x2.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q1-11": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>11. Q1-11 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A level-only exponential-smoothing forecast is 80 units. A single unusually high observation of 100 arrives; the update uses alpha=.25. Which set of conclusions is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The new level is 85; the update reacts partly to the new observation, and a single update does not prove a permanent shift</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The update is .25*100+.75*80=85. Smoothing mixes new information with the existing level. Neither the fitted level nor one unusual observation establishes future certainty or a sustained structural change.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-12": {"key": "ABCD", "html": "<h2>12. Q1-12 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two defect classifiers are evaluated on the same 1,000 representative cases. A has 3 missed defects and 10 false alarms; B has 1 missed defect and 30 false alarms. A miss costs $100, a false alarm costs $2, and correct decisions are free. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A makes fewer total errors but has higher stated total error cost than B; B. The preferred classifier can change if the stated error costs change; C. A costs $320 on this evaluation set; D. B costs $160 on this evaluation set</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A costs 3*100+10*2=320; B costs 1*100+30*2=160. A makes 13 errors and B 31, but B has the lower stated cost. Error types and their consequences matter, and changing costs can reverse the ranking.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision consequences and expected cost:</strong> Define the positive outcome, count the two error types, and weight them by their consequences. For one probability-based decision, compare the expected cost of the two actions. Do not assume 50% is universally the right threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q1-13": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>13. Q1-13 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A manufacturer records operating hours before failure once for each successive item. Batches contain exactly 120 items but take 5 to 7 calendar days. The question concerns patterns by position in each batch. Which setup fits the observation index?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Use operating hours before failure as x_t and 120 observations as the seasonal period</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The response is reliability in operating hours, while the index advances once per manufactured item. The repeatable cycle is 120 item observations. Calendar duration does not define the seasonal period for this index.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-14": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>14. Q1-14 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression reports a positive temperature coefficient with a confidence interval entirely above zero. The data are observational, and temperature changes along with production speed. Which conclusion is strongest without additional causal evidence?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Temperature has a supported positive association under the fitted specification, but changing temperature alone is not proved to cause that response change</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Coefficient output describes the fitted conditional association under its assumptions. Correlated operating conditions and omitted causes can complicate interpretation. Statistical evidence for an association neither proves an intervention effect nor validates the entire model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Regression uncertainty, association, and practical value:</strong> Interpret coefficients conditional on the included predictors, with units and uncertainty. Significance does not establish causality or practical importance; an interval including zero does not prove no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q1-15": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>15. Q1-15 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A safety classifier deliberately accepts more training misclassifications to obtain a wider-margin solution. A colleague says an SVM must always prefer whichever classifier makes the fewest training errors. Which answer best reflects the lecture tradeoff?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Soft-margin SVM balances fit and margin; the setting should be compared using validation and the relevant error consequences</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Soft-margin classification allows a tradeoff between fitting training cases and the margin/regularization penalty. Training perfection is not its only objective. Neither a wide margin nor low training error guarantees generalization, especially when false positives and false negatives have different costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-16": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>16. Q1-16 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An analyst says “we use regression,” then names predictors, then supplies fitted coefficients. Which statements about the word model are valid?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It can mean a method family; C. It can mean a predictor specification; D. It can mean a fitted equation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lectures use model at all three levels. Clarify whether a question concerns the family, specification, or fitted numerical instance.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q1-17": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>17. Q1-17 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A screening model labels disease as positive. On 100 patients it yields TP=36, FN=4, FP=12, TN=48. A doctor asks both how often diseased patients are caught and how often a positive result is actually disease. Which statements answer those two questions?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The fraction of diseased patients caught is 36/40; D. The fraction of positive predictions that are actual disease is 36/48</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Actual disease cases are TP+FN=40; positive predictions are TP+FP=48. The two conditional questions therefore have different denominators. False negatives are genuinely diseased but predicted negative.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q1-18": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>18. Q1-18 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about ordinary PCA are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It uses predictors without response labels; B. It produces orthogonal loading directions; C. It ranks components by predictor variance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA is unsupervised. Maximizing predictor variance and maximizing usefulness for a response are different objectives.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-19": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>19. Q1-19 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A hospital predicts tomorrow&#x27;s arrivals, estimates waiting time under alternative staffing levels, and must choose a schedule subject to a staffing budget. Which statements correctly distinguish the steps?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The final constrained staffing choice is prescriptive; B. The objectives and staffing constraints matter beyond the arrival prediction; C. Forecasting arrivals is a predictive input to the decision</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Predictions provide information about likely outcomes. Choosing among feasible actions requires the objective, constraints, and consequences. A forecast alone does not choose a staffing decision or guarantee that its downstream policy is best.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q1-20": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>20. Q1-20 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In upper CUSUM, which changes generally make the alarm less sensitive?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Increase the threshold T; B. Increase the allowance C</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A larger allowance reduces accumulated evidence and a larger threshold requires more evidence. Resetting every period discards the cumulative mechanism rather than tuning it correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q1-21": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>21. Q1-21 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A positive-valued cost response has a strongly skewed distribution and a regression shows increasing error spread at higher fitted values. A team proposes a Box-Cox transformation. Which statements are defensible at the lecture&#x27;s level?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Model diagnostics and validation still matter after the transformation; C. The transformation can be considered to improve the suitability of the response for modeling; D. Choosing the logarithmic member requires the response to be suitable for a log transformation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Box-Cox is a response-transformation family, including a logarithmic case, used to address shape and modeling behavior. Positivity is relevant to this family. A transformation does not guarantee a distributional shape, causal interpretation, or valid predictive model; the resulting model must still be evaluated.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Transformations and response scale:</strong> A transformation can improve model behavior but does not guarantee a correct specification. Distinguish predictor transformations, response transformations, and detrending. Report predictions and coefficients on the appropriate scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q1-22": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>22. Q1-22 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Optimal WSS drops as k rises all the way to one cluster per distinct point. Why does selecting the smallest WSS alone fail?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. It mechanically favors increasingly fine partitions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> At the extreme, each distinct observation can be its own center with zero WSS. Use marginal improvement and practical requirements instead of raw fit alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-23": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>23. Q1-23 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two regression specifications are fit to the same response on the same observations. The more complex model has a better likelihood but a higher AIC. What is the best interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its gain in fit did not offset AIC&#x27;s complexity penalty, so AIC favors the lower-score model</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> AIC combines likelihood with a penalty for fitted parameters, and lower is preferred for a comparable comparison. Better fit alone need not compensate for added complexity. The criterion does not establish causality or universal predictive superiority.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q1-24": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>24. Q1-24 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Income ranges in millions while a credit measure ranges in hundreds. Which preparations can make distances more comparable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Standardize each feature using training statistics; B. Evaluate whether feature weights reflect the intended notion of similarity; D. Scale each feature using its training range</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Scaling addresses units, and meaningful weighting addresses substantive importance. Increasing the already large scale worsens numerical dominance without justification.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q1-25": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>25. Q1-25 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A large tree improves training accuracy by isolating one observation in a leaf. What conclusions are warranted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Validation or pruning can assess whether the split helps; C. That split is a warning sign for overfitting; D. The training improvement alone does not prove better generalization</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A singleton leaf can fit noise. The sample quiz treats this as an overfitting concern; an extra feature or smaller training error is insufficient evidence of value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q1-26": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>26. Q1-26 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A city is choosing emergency-response stations. Candidate A is closer to many incidents by straight-line distance but separated by a river; candidate B has a longer straight-line distance but a direct road route. Which conclusion best follows?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The distance used in the analysis should represent travel constraints; Euclidean closeness alone need not imply quicker response</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Geographic grouping can inform the plan, but operational distance and feasibility matter. Straight-line distance ignores barriers and road routes. The information supports changing or checking the representation, not declaring a winner without travel evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-27": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>27. Q1-27 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A single extreme production record is found. Which actions are justified before deciding whether to exclude it?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Assess whether the intended model should cover that condition; C. Investigate sensor or transcription problems; D. Check whether an unusual real operating condition occurred</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Outlier handling depends on validity and relevance. Removing a meaningful adverse event simply to improve fit can produce unrealistic predictions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-28": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>28. Q1-28 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A financial team needs both tomorrow&#x27;s expected return and how variable that return may be. Which statements distinguish the lecture methods?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Mean and variance forecasts answer different planning questions; B. GARCH addresses time-varying variance; D. ARIMA can address a forecast of the series value</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> ARIMA and smoothing target values; GARCH targets conditional variance using a related lagged structure. Variance expresses variability rather than return direction. Modeling both outputs may be useful, but one is not a substitute for the other.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q1-29": {"key": "BC", "html": "<h2>29. Q1-29 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An additive wage regression gives an age coefficient of $500 per year while holding education and other included attributes fixed. Older employees in the data also tend to have more education. Which statements are supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The coefficient describes the fitted age association conditional on the included predictors; C. It does not by itself prove that making an employee a year older causes a $500 wage increase</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> An additive coefficient is a conditional model comparison, not an unconditional comparison of different people or an intervention effect. Interactions allow an association to vary with another attribute. Correlation between predictors is a reason to be careful, not a proof that the coefficient is causal.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q1-30": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>30. Q1-30 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A nearest-neighbor loan model treats ZIP code as a numeric coordinate. It concludes that ZIP codes 10001 and 10002 represent more similar borrowers than 10001 and 90210 solely because their numerical difference is smaller. What is the central modeling problem?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A numeric-looking category identifier does not automatically define a meaningful distance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> An identifier can be categorical even when stored as digits. Scaling changes numerical magnitude but does not create meaningful semantic distance. Any representation must match the intended notion of borrower similarity.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-31": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>31. Q1-31 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which regressions are linear in their fitted coefficients?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. log(y)=b0+b1*x; B. y=b0+b1*x+b2*x squared; D. y=b0+b1*x1+b2*x1*x2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Transforming predictors or the response does not destroy linearity in coefficients when coefficients multiply fixed columns. A coefficient inside an exponential makes the last form nonlinear in that coefficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q1-32": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>32. Q1-32 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In one k-means iteration, a few remote customer locations are assigned to a cluster with a dense central group. Which statements describe the standard mean-center update and its interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The remote locations can pull the center away from the dense group; D. The new center is the mean of the currently assigned coordinates</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> K-means uses the arithmetic mean for a fixed membership under its squared-distance objective. Remote points can influence that mean, which need not be an observed or physically feasible location. A centroid is an analytic result, not a complete facility decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-33": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>33. Q1-33 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A retailer operating five days weekly assigns every second record to validation in a fixed five-record cycle. What is the central concern?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The split may isolate a particular weekday</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Rotation can synchronize with seasonality and omit weekdays from training. Representative allocation within relevant groups is preferable to unexamined systematic rotation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-34": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>34. Q1-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team fits many trees that all tend to choose the same dominant predictor first and make similar mistakes. In the lecture&#x27;s random-forest idea, why restrict which predictors a tree or split may consider?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. To introduce diversity so the combined prediction can benefit from different strengths and weaknesses</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Restricting candidate predictors introduces randomness and reduces uniform dependence on one predictor. The ensemble combines varied models; it is still supervised and still requires evaluation. Random exclusion is not a declaration that an attribute is irrelevant.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests and diverse trees:</strong> Know how randomized predictor choices can diversify supervised trees and why combining different strengths may help. Averaging or voting does not repair leakage or prove causality. Bootstrap/OOB calculations are not required for this bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-35": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>35. Q1-35 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A temperature is normal in summer but extremely unusual for its recorded winter date. Which outlier type best describes it?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Contextual</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The abnormality depends on seasonal context. A value need not be globally extreme to be unusual at a particular time.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-36": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>36. Q1-36 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which interpretations of ARIMA(2,1,3) are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Two autoregressive lags are included; B. Three lagged innovations are included; C. The series is differenced once</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The order tuple is p,d,q: lagged values, differencing order, lagged errors. These are distinct from clustering or seasonal cycle length.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q1-37": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>37. Q1-37 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A three-class k-NN rule sees five neighbors: red, red, blue, green, blue. What additional detail is needed to return a unique class?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A tie-breaking rule</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Red and blue each have two votes. Multi-class voting can tie even with odd k; the algorithm must specify how to resolve it.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-38": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>38. Q1-38 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about a p-value of .01 for a coefficient are valid?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It can indicate evidence against a zero-coefficient null; D. It is computed assuming the tested null and statistical model</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A p-value is a tail probability under a null, not a posterior probability of that null. Statistical evidence and practical magnitude are different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Regression uncertainty, association, and practical value:</strong> Interpret coefficients conditional on the included predictors, with units and uncertainty. Significance does not establish causality or practical importance; an interval including zero does not prove no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-39": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>39. Q1-39 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team uses the first two principal components, which explain 90% of predictor variance, before fitting a classification model. Which statements are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The omitted low-variance direction might still contain useful class information; B. The resulting predictive procedure should be validated rather than judged solely by explained predictor variance; C. The retained components summarize 90% of the variation in the predictors under the fitted scaling</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA ranks directions by predictor variance, without using the response. Predictor variance explained is neither accuracy nor response variance explained. A smaller component can carry predictive information, so the complete pipeline needs validation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-40": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>40. Q1-40 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements describe standard Euclidean k-means correctly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It uses no known response labels; C. Multiple initializations can produce different final solutions; D. It repeatedly assigns points and updates means</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The alternating procedure is a fast heuristic and can stop at local optima. Repeated starts improve the opportunity to find a better solution, not a proof of global optimality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-41": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>41. Q1-41 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> After CV chooses a model specification, which actions are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Evaluate the refitted model on reserved test data; B. Refit the chosen specification on all non-test data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cross-validation selects a specification. The lecture recommends a fresh fit on the available non-test data rather than selecting or averaging the intermediate fitted coefficients.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-42": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>42. Q1-42 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An upper CUSUM monitor has baseline 10, allowance 2, previous statistic 4, and new observation 15. Its rule alarms when the updated statistic is greater than 6. Which interpretation is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. The statistic becomes 7 and an alarm is triggered; the alarm supports investigation of an upward change but not a particular cause</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The update is max(0,4+15-10-2)=7, which exceeds 6. Accumulated evidence supports a change signal under the specified settings; it does not identify a cause or ensure a permanent future pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q1-43": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>43. Q1-43 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A multiplicative seasonal factor for early-batch parts is .8 and the response is operating hours before failure. What conclusion is supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Early-batch parts have lower expected operating hours than the adjusted baseline</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A factor below one reduces expected response by a proportion. It suggests earlier failure but does not establish a mechanism or an additive 80-hour difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-44": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>44. Q1-44 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team reserves 200 of 1,000 observations for a final test and uses four-fold validation on the other 800 to compare SVM settings. Which statements describe the fitting stages correctly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. After selection, the chosen specification can be refit on all 800 non-test observations; D. Each equal-sized fold fit uses 600 training observations and 200 validation observations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Four-fold validation repeatedly fits on three of the four 200-observation folds. It chooses the specification, followed by refitting on all non-test data. Selecting a lucky fold model or tuning on the test set confuses the data roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q1-45": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>45. Q1-45 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Ten candidate models are fit on training data and the best validation performer is chosen. Which dataset should provide the final reported estimate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. An untouched test set</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Selection can capitalize on validation randomness. A test set independent of fitting and selection estimates performance of the selected procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-46": {"key": "0; 1", "html": "<h2>46. Q1-46 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> After subtracting a feature&#x27;s mean and dividing by its standard deviation, the transformed feature has mean ____ and standard deviation ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0; 1</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> This is standardization. It changes location and scale but does not guarantee a normal distribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-47": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>47. Q1-47 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A test set remains an independent final evaluation set even if its labels were used to choose the best threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Threshold selection uses test outcomes and turns that dataset into selection data. A fresh independent evaluation is then needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-48": {"key": "supervised", "html": "<h2>48. Q1-48 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A k-means grouping was learned without response labels. Assigning a new observation to its closest learned center does not change the original fit into ____ learning.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> supervised</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The clustering fit remains unsupervised because it used feature information without known response labels. New-point assignment uses the learned representation rather than supplying outcome labels.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q1-49": {"key": "True", "html": "<h2>49. Q1-49 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For ordinary Holt forecasting, the h-step-ahead forecast is current level plus h times current trend.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> True</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The future baseline extends the current estimated per-period trend across h periods.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q1-50": {"key": "0.75; 75%", "html": "<h2>50. Q1-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For TP=36 and FP=12, precision is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.75; 75%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Precision is TP/(TP+FP)=36/48=.75; it answers how many positive predictions are correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q2-01": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>1. Q2-01 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which reasons can justify kernel SVM rather than a strictly linear separator?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The feature-label relationship has a curved boundary; C. A nonlinear separator may fit patterns missed by a straight hyperplane; D. Suitable kernel settings improve validation performance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Kernels expand the available boundary shapes. Their usefulness must be evaluated; more flexible boundaries do not establish causality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-02": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>2. Q2-02 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A customer-segmentation run reports WSS=26 for k=1 and WSS=0 when every observed customer has a separate center. A manager selects the second run solely because zero looks perfect. Which criticisms are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. An operationally useful k may be smaller even with positive WSS; C. WSS rewards extra clusters and does not by itself establish a useful segmentation; D. An elbow and the intended use can help assess whether added clusters are worthwhile</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> One center per distinct observation can drive training WSS to zero without discovering useful groups. Compare the amount of improvement and the purpose, such as service capacity or interpretable segments. WSS is not a supervised accuracy measure or a guarantee for new cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-03": {"key": "BC", "html": "<h2>3. Q2-03 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Daily orders show both an increasing baseline and a repeatable seven-day pattern. A Holt-Winters update is processing Tuesday. Which statements correctly distinguish its components?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The seasonal comparison uses the previous estimate for the same weekday; C. A Tuesday seasonal factor above one indicates a response above the current baseline in the multiplicative formulation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The seasonal state tracks the same phase of the observation cycle; trend tracks change across observations. A multiplicative factor is relative to the baseline. The response units do not define the observation count in a cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-04": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>4. Q2-04 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A random forest and a single classification tree are trained on labeled claims. The forest lets different trees consider different predictor sets and combines their class predictions. Which claims are defensible at the lecture&#x27;s level?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Combining diverse trees can improve predictive performance but still requires validation; D. The randomized predictor sets can produce trees with different strengths</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The randomization is intended to diversify supervised models whose predictions are combined. It does not remove the need for training responses or performance evaluation. An ensemble is generally harder to summarize as a single transparent decision path.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests and diverse trees:</strong> Know how randomized predictor choices can diversify supervised trees and why combining different strengths may help. Averaging or voting does not repair leakage or prove causality. Bootstrap/OOB calculations are not required for this bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q2-05": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>5. Q2-05 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish additive from multiplicative seasonality?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Multiplicative seasonality represents a proportion of level; B. A factor of 1 indicates no multiplicative seasonal change; D. Additive seasonality represents a unit offset</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Multiplicative updates divide by seasonal factors, so zeros are problematic. Additive and proportional effects describe different relationships to the level.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-06": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>6. Q2-06 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A sales team has an ordered response series with a changing mean. After one differencing step, it wants to use two previous modeled values and one previous prediction error. Which ARIMA description matches that specification?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. ARIMA(2,1,1): two autoregressive lags, one differencing step, one error lag</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The orders identify autoregressive value lags p, differencing d, and lagged errors q in that order. A changing mean motivates the stated differencing step, not an automatic change caused by p. GARCH targets variance rather than the sales value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a> · <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q2-07": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>7. Q2-07 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A flexible spline has training MSE zero and much higher held-out MSE than a straight-line model. What is the best interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The spline may fit dataset-specific noise</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Exact interpolation can overfit. Independent predictive performance is more informative than training perfection.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-08": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>8. Q2-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A factory uses single exponential smoothing. During a persistent upward shift, a setting with very small alpha changes its forecast only slowly. Which conclusions follow?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A larger alpha would put more weight on the latest observation; B. Small alpha gives more persistence to the previous level</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Alpha controls the response to new observations in the level update. A higher value responds faster but can follow noise more strongly; it does not add trend/seasonality or guarantee predictive quality. Tuning requires the relevant validation evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-09": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>9. Q2-09 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For one repair request, three regression trees predict 8, 10, and 15 hours. The combined forest averages their outputs. Which conclusion is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. It predicts 11 hours; averaging does not prove the actual repair time or a causal explanation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The mean is (8+10+15)/3=11. Diversity is precisely why tree predictions can differ. This is a numeric ensemble prediction, not an individual-outcome guarantee or causal claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests and diverse trees:</strong> Know how randomized predictor choices can diversify supervised trees and why combining different strengths may help. Averaging or voting does not repair leakage or prove causality. Bootstrap/OOB calculations are not required for this bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-10": {"key": "AC", "html": "<h2>10. Q2-10 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A retailer believes marketing emails affect spending differently for younger and older customers. Its current additive regression imposes one common email coefficient. Which lecture-level revisions could represent that variation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Use a tree to define groups with different fitted responses or local relationships; C. Add an email-by-age interaction to the regression</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Interactions and tree-defined regions can represent relationships that vary across groups. Changing one common coefficient still imposes one common additive effect. Additional flexibility requires evaluation to avoid fitting noise, and observational email associations are not automatically causal.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-11": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>11. Q2-11 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A restaurant first predicts next week&#x27;s demand, then chooses purchase quantities under spoilage and shortage costs. What is the role of the demand forecast?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. A predictive input to a prescriptive decision</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Expected demand supports an inventory decision, but quantities also depend on costs, constraints, and uncertainty. Prediction and prescription are connected stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q2-12": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>12. Q2-12 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A Holt forecast has level 100 and trend -3 response units per observation. The team wants a four-observation-ahead point forecast. Which interpretation is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The forecast is 88, extending the estimated trend, not guaranteeing four future observations will fall by exactly three</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Ordinary Holt extends the current trend: 100+4*(-3)=88. The trend is in response units per index step. It is an estimated pattern, not a deterministic sequence or evidence of the cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-13": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>13. Q2-13 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A new point is equally far from two learned centers. What is needed for deterministic cluster assignment?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A tie policy</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Nearest-center assignment can tie. A documented tie policy yields reproducible assignment without changing the unsupervised training task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-14": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>14. Q2-14 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Demand forecasts from a mean model are reasonably centered, but forecast errors have periods of small variability and periods of large variability. Management wants to size a reserve against uncertainty. Which statements are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A larger variance estimate implies greater modeled variability, not necessarily a higher demand mean; B. The mean forecast can remain useful even when a separate variance model is needed; C. A GARCH model can address changing variance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture distinguishes forecasting values from forecasting variance. A variability model can inform uncertainty planning without replacing the mean model. Variance does not specify error sign or prove the expected value rises.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a></p>"}, "Q2-15": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>15. Q2-15 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A seven-neighbor classifier finds labels A,A,A,B,B,C,C. An analyst rejects the result because no class has a majority. Which response best matches unweighted multiclass k-NN?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Class A wins by plurality; the highest vote count need not exceed half of k</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A has three votes, exceeding B and C with two each. Classification uses the most frequent label; a strict majority is not required in a multiclass problem. Averaging numeric outcomes describes k-NN regression, not this task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-16": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>16. Q2-16 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about k-means cluster centers are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. They need not be observed data points; C. An extreme point can pull a center; D. They are coordinatewise means under the standard objective</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Centers summarize feature values learned from memberships. They are not supervised labels and may lie between observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-17": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>17. Q2-17 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A rescue robot must finish moving on both axes before it can act. The required horizontal and vertical travel times are 5 and 12 seconds, and the motions occur simultaneously. A teammate uses Euclidean distance because it gives 13. What is the best correction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The completion time is 12 seconds under simultaneous independent motions; the maximum, not the Euclidean length, matches this task</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The slower concurrent movement determines when both are finished. L-infinity on time coordinates captures that operational requirement. Sequential movements would instead sum, while Euclidean distance describes a different geometric notion.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-18": {"key": "AC", "html": "<h2>18. Q2-18 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A loan team has amount, payment method, repayment outcome, and a late-fee total recorded after the repayment deadline. It wants to predict repayment before approving a new loan. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Post-deadline late fees should not be treated as information available at approval; C. Repayment outcome supplies historical labels for supervised learning</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The observed outcome is the response used to train the procedure. Predictors must be available when the decision is made; a later consequence can leak the answer. Splitting or changing the model family does not repair invalid prediction-time information.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a></p>"}, "Q2-19": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>19. Q2-19 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> When comparing repeated k-means starts at the same k, which practices make sense?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Ensure the distance and scaling are consistent across runs; B. Recognize that different starts can converge to the same solution; D. Keep the solution with lower WSS on the same prepared data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cluster labels are arbitrary; changing label names does not change fit. Compare a common objective under the same representation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-20": {"key": "BC", "html": "<h2>20. Q2-20 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression is fitted by minimizing squared prediction errors. Its modeling argument assumes independent, identically distributed normal errors. Which statements connect this to the lecture&#x27;s likelihood discussion?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The error assumptions are part of why that equivalence holds; C. Under those assumptions, coefficient estimates minimizing squared errors also maximize the corresponding likelihood</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The equivalence follows from the stated Gaussian error model. It is a conditional mathematical relationship, not evidence that the assumptions hold in every dataset. Likelihood alone can reward flexibility, which motivates complexity penalties and validation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-21": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>21. Q2-21 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish underfitting from overfitting?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Validation can help choose an appropriate flexibility level; C. An overly flexible form may fit random structure; D. An overly restrictive form may miss real structure</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Underfitting misses signal and overfitting follows noise. Held-out evaluation helps assess the tradeoff rather than maximizing training fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-22": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>22. Q2-22 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A CUSUM analysis of temperatures signals that a warm period lasted longer in one region. A report attributes the signal to one newly implemented policy. Which evaluation is most defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. The signal depends on the baseline, settings, and data definition; it does not by itself identify the policy as the cause</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A change-detection output is conditional on what was measured and how the monitor was specified. Honest interpretation reports those choices and avoids attributing causes unsupported by the analysis. Selecting definitions for a desired result undermines the claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q2-23": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>23. Q2-23 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A feature&#x27;s training minimum and maximum are 20 and 60. A new case has value 70. The team applies the same min-max transformation used in training. Which conclusions follow?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. A value above one can occur because the new case lies outside the fitted training range; D. The new transformed value is 1.25</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The value is (70-20)/(60-20)=1.25. Training-range bounds are not guarantees for future observations. Reusing the training transformation keeps representations consistent; a real extreme case must be investigated rather than automatically declared invalid.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-24": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>24. Q2-24 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two soft-margin SVM settings are tested on the same held-out defect cases. Setting A has more training errors but fewer held-out missed defects and lower stated total decision cost than B. Which recommendation best follows?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Favor A for the stated objective, subject to an independent final evaluation after selection</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The objective is the stated deployment cost, and held-out selection evidence favors A. Training perfection is not decisive. After choosing among settings, a final untouched evaluation is still needed rather than treating selection performance as unbiased final evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-25": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>25. Q2-25 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two analysts use the symbol lambda for different SVM tradeoffs. In one stated objective lambda multiplies the margin-related coefficient penalty; in the other it multiplies the fitting-loss penalty. Which conclusion is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. One cannot infer the same tuning direction from the symbol alone; inspect the term it multiplies</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Parameter effects depend on their role in the actual specification. Weighting the fitting penalty and weighting the coefficient penalty change the balance in opposite ways. The name alone does not establish a tuning rule or the learning task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-26": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>26. Q2-26 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A hard-margin SVM illustration shows two parallel margin boundaries with the closest class points touching them. Another separator also classifies all training points correctly but has a narrower margin. What feature makes the maximum-margin solution preferred under this model&#x27;s objective?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Its wider separation from the nearest class points, rather than merely the count of correctly classified training cases</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The hard-margin idea maximizes the separating margin among feasible separators. Equal training accuracy does not imply equal objective quality. This geometric criterion is not a probability output or a guarantee of future accuracy.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-27": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>27. Q2-27 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> What information is needed to tune change-detection settings sensibly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Costs of false alarms; C. Representative behavior before and after changes; D. Costs of detection delay</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Sensitivity choices depend on operational tradeoffs and representative data. Unnecessary stops or repeated alarms can impose substantial costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q2-28": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>28. Q2-28 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which steps correctly detrend a response for factor-based modeling?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Add back the relevant trend when recovering original-scale predictions; B. Estimate an appropriate time trend on the permitted training data; C. Subtract that fitted trend from observed responses</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Detrending removes the selected trend component. Seasonality and other dependence may remain, and original-scale forecasts need the reverse adjustment.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Transformations and response scale:</strong> A transformation can improve model behavior but does not guarantee a correct specification. Distinguish predictor transformations, response transformations, and detrending. Report predictions and coefficients on the appropriate scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-29": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>29. Q2-29 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about increasing regression complexity are correct for nested least-squares fits with an intercept on the same training data?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Adjusted R-squared may decrease; C. Training R-squared cannot decrease; D. Training SSE cannot increase</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> More columns expand the possible fitted responses, so training fit cannot worsen. A complexity-adjusted metric or independent predictive score can worsen.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-30": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>30. Q2-30 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A feature is standardized to z=(x-50)/10. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. x=70 maps to z=2; D. x=30 maps to z=-2</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Standardization expresses distance from the mean in standard-deviation units. It neither bounds the values nor changes the distribution into a Gaussian shape.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q2-31": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>31. Q2-31 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A repair tree first asks whether machine age is below 30 months. For the older branch it then asks whether load is below 50 units. A new machine is 40 months old with load 60. The reached leaf has only two training cases. Which statements are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Use the response estimate associated with the reached leaf; B. Follow the older-machine branch and then the load-at-least-50 branch; C. The very small leaf warrants concern about the stability of its prediction</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Prediction follows the successive conditions for the new case. A single tree uses the reached leaf; a forest combines different trees. A two-case leaf can be highly sensitive to training noise and should be checked through validation and size controls.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q2-32": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>32. Q2-32 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two centered and standardized predictors measure production position and elapsed production day. They are highly positively correlated. PCA produces a dominant first component. Which interpretation is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Most predictor variation lies along a shared direction; this does not prove that component is the best predictor of failure time</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Correlated standardized predictors share a large direction of variation. PCA ranks predictor variance without response information. It does not establish predictive performance, response variance explained, or a causal mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-33": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>33. Q2-33 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A logistic model predicts 0.70 probability of repayment for one applicant, and the lending rule grants a loan at 0.80 or above. Which statements distinguish the outputs?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A different decision threshold can change the action without changing the fitted probability for this applicant; C. Under the stated rule the applicant is classified below the loan-granting threshold; D. The probability estimate is 0.70</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The fitted probability and the rule using it are distinct. Since .70&lt;.80, this rule does not grant the loan. Altering the action threshold changes decisions, not the existing probability estimate. Logistic probability is not a currency-valued response.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Logistic probability and classification:</strong> Separate a fitted binary-event probability from the class or action produced by a threshold. The threshold can change without refitting probabilities, and its value depends on error consequences.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-34": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>34. Q2-34 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A time series has a drifting mean, while differences between consecutive observations fluctuate around a much more stable mean. Which ARIMA choice addresses the described feature most directly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A differencing step, with its adequacy checked on the resulting series</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The differencing component addresses nonstationary level behavior when the differenced process is more stable. Value lags and error lags play different roles, and no order should be assumed to guarantee suitability. Variance modeling and PCA answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q2-35": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>35. Q2-35 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A forecasting model will use data through Friday to predict Saturday. Which evaluation design best mirrors deployment?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Train on earlier periods and validate on later periods</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A chronological evaluation reproduces what information will exist at forecast time. Random splits can allow future patterns or preprocessing information into training.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Forecast validation without future information:</strong> Use training windows that precede their forecast targets. Distinguish forecast errors from in-sample residuals. A rolling origin evaluates forecasts at repeated historical cutoffs, using only information available at each cutoff.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/tscv.html\">Forecasting textbook: time-series validation</a></p>"}, "Q2-36": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>36. Q2-36 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about large residuals are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. They can indicate a missing predictor or wrong model form; C. They merit investigation in the intended application; D. They can indicate data errors</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Residuals measure discrepancy from the fitted model, which itself may be incomplete or misspecified. They are evidence to examine rather than proof of bad data.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-37": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>37. Q2-37 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which preprocessing practices avoid leakage in cross-validation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Estimate feature means on each fold&#x27;s training portion; C. Apply those fitted means to the held-out fold; D. Select predictors using only the fold&#x27;s training outcomes</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Preprocessing and feature selection are parts of fitting. Letting held-out observations influence them makes validation more optimistic.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-38": {"key": "BC", "html": "<h2>38. Q2-38 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which conclusions follow from retaining all orthonormal PCA components of a feature space?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The representation changes coordinates; C. No information is discarded solely by the full rotation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA only reduces dimensionality when components are omitted. A complete orthogonal transformation preserves the available feature information.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-39": {"key": "BC", "html": "<h2>39. Q2-39 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two regressions predict a numeric response on the same held-out observations. A has lower held-out squared error but uses a sensor that becomes available only after the decision; B has slightly higher error using timely attributes. Which conclusions follow?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Prediction-time feasibility can favor B despite its higher measured error; C. A&#x27;s reported error does not establish that it can serve the stated decision time</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Accuracy must be assessed under the information and timing available in deployment. A future or delayed sensor can make an apparently strong result unusable. A different model family does not correct unavailable inputs; the representation must change.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-40": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>40. Q2-40 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about ARIMA&#x27;s moving-average component are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its order q counts innovation lags; B. It can coexist with an autoregressive component; C. It uses past innovations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The MA terminology here refers to errors entering the time-series equation. It is not a sliding arithmetic average of observed levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q2-41": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>41. Q2-41 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two model families are compared using five equally sized validation folds. A averages error 6 but varies markedly between folds; B averages 6.2 with more stable results. Which claim is most appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. A has the lower reported average, but the small difference and variation warrant caution and a separate final evaluation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cross-validation summarizes selection evidence, which can vary with the data split. A small average advantage is not a universal guarantee. The selected specification is refit and evaluated separately; one lucky fold fit is not the default final model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-42": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>42. Q2-42 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For upper CUSUM, previous S=3, x=7, mu=10, C=1. What is the new statistic?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 0</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The raw sum is 3+7-10-1=-1, so reset to zero. Carrying negative debt would delay detection of a later increase.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q2-43": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>43. Q2-43 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about inference and prediction are reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. A less interpretable method may be useful when predictive accuracy is the priority; D. An interpretable model can help explain modeled associations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Model complexity can reduce interpretability and may overfit. Neither flexibility nor accurate prediction guarantees causal explanation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q2-44": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>44. Q2-44 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A heartbeat sequence lacks one expected beat, but none of the observed amplitudes is unusual. Which representation can expose a point outlier?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Time between consecutive beats</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A missed beat creates an unusually long interbeat interval. Transforming the representation can reveal the anomaly as an extreme individual value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-45": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>45. Q2-45 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression has R-squared=.60 on its training data. Adding predictors raises it to .70, but held-out prediction error increases. Which statements are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The additional predictors may be fitting patterns that do not generalize; C. The training fit improved by this measure; D. Held-out error and complexity considerations matter when selecting the specification</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Training R-squared describes fit on the data used to estimate the model. Additional flexibility can improve it while hurting validation performance. Selection should consider the prediction objective and complexity rather than treating a training fit increase as proof of generalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q2-46": {"key": "14", "html": "<h2>46. Q2-46 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For coordinate differences of 6 and 8, Manhattan distance is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 14</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> L1 adds absolute differences: 6+8=14.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-47": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>47. Q2-47 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If removing an outlier improves fit, that alone proves removal is appropriate for the intended application.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Improved fit is not evidence that the record is invalid or irrelevant. Investigate origin and whether the target use must represent rare events.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-48": {"key": "246", "html": "<h2>48. Q2-48 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With level=200, trend=5, and the next multiplicative seasonal factor=1.2, the one-step forecast is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 246</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Multiply the projected baseline by the seasonal factor: (200+5)*1.2=246.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q2-49": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>49. Q2-49 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A low p-value is sufficient evidence that changing the predictor will cause the estimated change in the response.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A low p-value can support a modeled association. Confounding, reverse causation, and design limitations still matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Regression uncertainty, association, and practical value:</strong> Interpret coefficients conditional on the included predictors, with units and uncertainty. Significance does not establish causality or practical importance; an interval including zero does not prove no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q2-50": {"key": "0.75; 75%", "html": "<h2>50. Q2-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If a classification leaf has 9 positive and 3 negative training cases, its empirical positive fraction is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.75; 75%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The fraction is 9/(9+3)=.75. The majority class is positive, while .75 is a probability estimate rather than a causal claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-01": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>1. Q3-01 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which split designs can address nonrepresentative subsets?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Stratify by important classes when appropriate; B. Check whether systematic rotation aligns with periodic structure; C. Consider relevant regimes or groups</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Randomization is useful but does not guarantee rare-case coverage. Design should match the population, dependencies, and use case.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-02": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>2. Q3-02 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression coefficient is estimated at -4, with a reported 95% confidence interval from -9 to 1. The manager says this proves there is no association and proposes deleting the predictor without checking prediction quality. Which statements are warranted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The estimate is negative, but the reported uncertainty includes positive and negative values; C. Predictive usefulness should be evaluated rather than inferred solely from whether the interval includes zero; D. The interval includes zero, so it does not give strong evidence against a zero coefficient at the matching conventional level</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The interval reflects uncertainty around the coefficient under the fitted model. Including zero is not proof of no relationship. Statistical significance, effect size, and predictive usefulness are different issues; a modeling decision should consider the task and validation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Regression uncertainty, association, and practical value:</strong> Interpret coefficients conditional on the included predictors, with units and uncertainty. Significance does not establish causality or practical importance; an interval including zero does not prove no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-03": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>3. Q3-03 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A sensor team has an ordered numeric series and two requests: forecast the next reading and alarm on a sustained increase from an established baseline. Which pair most directly assigns the lecture methods to those tasks?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. ARIMA or smoothing for the reading forecast, and CUSUM for accumulated evidence of an upward change</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Forecasting and change detection have different outputs. ARIMA/smoothing address values, while CUSUM accumulates evidence relative to a baseline. PCA and clustering are not direct substitutes; GARCH addresses variance, not the two stated value/mean-shift tasks.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q3-04": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>4. Q3-04 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two orthogonal PCA loadings produce a high-variance component unrelated to outcome and a low-variance component that separates classes. What does PCA alone recommend retaining first?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. The high-variance component</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Ordinary PCA ranks predictor variation without using labels. The example shows why dimension reduction can discard useful supervised information.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-05": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>5. Q3-05 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A scaled linear SVM assigns zero coefficient to x2, while a tree using the same labeled data splits on x2 in one region. Which conclusions are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. x2 can matter in a different representation or regional rule; B. x2 does not affect this particular fitted linear SVM score</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Zero coefficient has a precise meaning for this fitted score. Model-specific contribution is different from universal relevance. A tree can represent different regional structure, but using x2 does not prove its superiority or causality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-06": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>6. Q3-06 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two level-only smoothing models forecast weekly sales. One uses alpha=.8, the other .2. Last week had a one-off promotion unlikely to recur. Which statements correctly describe the tradeoff?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The promotion&#x27;s relevance to the forecast target should be investigated rather than judging settings solely by that week&#x27;s fit; B. The .8 update puts more weight on the promotion observation; C. The .2 update retains more of the previous level</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A higher alpha responds more strongly to the newest observation and can follow a transient event. A lower alpha is more persistent. Neither is universally best; the target population and validation determine which response to this event is appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-07": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>7. Q3-07 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which changes alter the substantive clustering problem?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Change the distance metric; B. Switch from miles to dollars on one dimension without scaling policy; D. Analyze day and night separately when their distributions differ</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Representation, geometry, and segmentation can change similarity. Pure label renaming does not change the partition.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-08": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>8. Q3-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A multiplicative Holt-Winters implementation begins with seasonal factors of one and initial trend zero. The analyst reports that the underlying process has no seasonality or trend because those are the starting values. What is the best correction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Initialization is a starting estimate; later observations update the states and can reveal trend or seasonal differences</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A seasonal factor of one is a neutral multiplicative starting effect; zero trend is a starting assumption. They are not permanent conclusions about the process. The smoothing updates revise states as data arrive.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-09": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>9. Q3-09 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A clinic monitors reductions in service time with lower CUSUM S_new=max(0,S_previous+mu-x-C). Baseline is 20, allowance 1, previous statistic 2, and new time 16. The alarm threshold is 6, triggered only when S_new&gt;6. Which conclusion is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. The statistic is 5; the low observation adds downward-change evidence but does not yet trigger this rule</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lower-direction update is max(0,2+20-16-1)=5. It accumulates evidence of lower values, and 5 does not exceed 6. The allowance reduces each increment; the monitor does not identify the cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-10": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>10. Q3-10 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A k-NN model has good training behavior but poor validation performance after adding many irrelevant dimensions. What is a sensible next step?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Evaluate feature selection or meaningful weighting inside validation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Irrelevant dimensions can distort proximity and weaken neighborhoods. Feature selection and weighting should be evaluated without contaminating the final test.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-11": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>11. Q3-11 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A classification tree splits a leaf into two small leaves and obtains perfect training labels in both. Held-out classification becomes worse. Which statements correctly interpret the result?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The split improved training fit; B. Pruning or increasing the support required for a split can be sensible responses; C. The new leaves may be too specialized to their training cases</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A split can isolate noise or rare training patterns. Held-out deterioration is evidence against selecting that additional flexibility for prediction. Pruning and adequate leaf support are lecture safeguards; training purity does not prove generalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-12": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>12. Q3-12 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A retailer compares an interpretable single tree with a random forest. The forest performs better on validation, but the retailer must explain each declined application through a short sequence of rules. Which conclusion follows?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Predictive performance and explanation requirements both matter; the forest&#x27;s validation gain does not by itself resolve the choice</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A single tree provides a direct rule path, while a forest combines models and may offer better accuracy with more complex interpretation. Selection should consider both the measurable prediction objective and operational requirements, rather than a universal ranking.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests and diverse trees:</strong> Know how randomized predictor choices can diversify supervised trees and why combining different strengths may help. Averaging or voting does not repair leakage or prove causality. Bootstrap/OOB calculations are not required for this bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-13": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>13. Q3-13 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team compares several SVM penalties and several k-NN neighborhood sizes using cross-validation. One SVM specification has the best mean validation result. Which actions are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Refit that selected specification using all non-test observations; B. Use an untouched final test set to assess the selected procedure</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cross-validation selects the procedure/settings, not a lucky fold fit. Refit the selected specification on all available non-test data, then test it independently. Selection performance has already influenced the choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q3-14": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>14. Q3-14 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> On the same dataset, model A has slightly worse likelihood but fewer parameters than B. A has lower AIC; B has lower unpenalized fitting error. Which conclusion is supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The criteria emphasize different tradeoffs, so B&#x27;s better fit does not refute AIC&#x27;s preference for A</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Penalized criteria combine fit and complexity, while unpenalized error measures fit alone. A model can sacrifice some training fit and obtain a better penalized score. This guides comparison without guaranteeing a causal or universally superior model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-15": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>15. Q3-15 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A k-means plan has WSS values 400, 210, 110, 98, and 90 for k=1 through 5. Managers can staff at most three service centers. Which interpretations make sense?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The curve suggests a candidate elbow near three clusters; C. The elbow is evidence to assess alongside service coverage, not proof that three is universally optimal; D. The operational limit supports considering three or fewer centers</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Large improvements continue through k=3, after which the gains are much smaller. An elbow suggests a candidate, but feasibility and coverage determine whether the plan is useful. Raw minimum WSS alone ignores both complexity and the staffing constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-16": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>16. Q3-16 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> PCA produces two components from centered, standardized production measurements. A loading combines several measurements, rather than identifying one original column to keep. Which distinctions are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. PCA is feature extraction because it creates combinations of the original features; D. The loadings describe directions used to compute component scores</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA transforms coordinates into component directions with loadings on the original features. It is not simply selecting columns or ranking feature means. The new representation may summarize variation efficiently but complicate interpretation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-17": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>17. Q3-17 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A rare toxic class is costly to miss. Which classification adjustments can be appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Evaluate the resulting false alarms and total cost; B. Use a lower toxic-probability threshold if miss costs dominate; D. Increase the penalty for missing toxic cases</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Unequal costs justify asymmetric decisions. A threshold is a decision policy, not a mathematical requirement of the probability model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision consequences and expected cost:</strong> Define the positive outcome, count the two error types, and weight them by their consequences. For one probability-based decision, compare the expected cost of the two actions. Do not assume 50% is universally the right threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-18": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>18. Q3-18 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression estimates a $0.001 spending difference per exposure unit with a very small p-value. The action needed to change exposure costs much more than the expected spending difference. Which statements are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The coefficient&#x27;s units and practical size matter; B. The cost comparison belongs to the decision question in addition to the modeling question; D. Statistical evidence for an association does not ensure economically valuable action</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Significance is not effect size, value, or causality. A statistically detectable small effect may be operationally negligible or costly to act on. The model informs decisions, but costs and intervention evidence remain relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Regression uncertainty, association, and practical value:</strong> Interpret coefficients conditional on the included predictors, with units and uncertainty. Significance does not establish causality or practical importance; an interval including zero does not prove no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-19": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>19. Q3-19 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A clustering analysis identifies a small but verified customer group far from most observations. Removing the group improves WSS and a regression&#x27;s fit. The business wants to serve all customer types. Which statements are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Better fit alone is not a sufficient reason to delete legitimate target customers; B. The group may reflect a meaningful segment rather than bad data; C. Separate analysis or reporting may help preserve the group&#x27;s operational importance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Outlier handling is driven by data validity and the intended population, not merely by a fit improvement. A real small segment can matter greatly to the business. Deleting it can create misleading performance and recommendations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-20": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>20. Q3-20 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression-tree split reduces within-node response variation, but one proposed child has very few observations. Which steps reflect the lecture&#x27;s branching and pruning ideas?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Use a quality-related splitting criterion together with safeguards against overfitting; C. Check whether the split improves estimation on separate data during pruning; D. Require enough data in each resulting leaf rather than splitting solely for a training improvement</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Branching seeks a useful quality improvement while preserving enough evidence in the children. Pruning checks whether added structure is beneficial on separate data. A tiny training-only improvement is not a sufficient reason to keep a split.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-21": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>21. Q3-21 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An airport needs expected arrival counts from time of day and holiday status. A second project has few labeled cases but useful expert beliefs about regression coefficients. Which pairing matches the survey introductions?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Poisson regression for the count-rate task; Bayesian regression to combine the second project&#x27;s prior information with data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Poisson regression lets the count mean/rate depend on attributes. Bayesian regression combines prior distributions with observations and updates them, rather than automatically trusting experts over data. Detailed fitting theory is unnecessary to distinguish these roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-22": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>22. Q3-22 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A classifier uses a horizontal boundary x2=5. Points above it are positive. A tree built from the same data uses both x1 and x2 and makes fewer training errors. Which statements are supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The tree may use a predictor that this particular linear rule does not use; D. x1 does not change the class under the stated horizontal rule</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The class depends on the vertical coordinate x2. Predictor usage is model-specific; a tree can represent a different rule. Training fit alone does not settle which model predicts better.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q3-23": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>23. Q3-23 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A sensor feature is constant in all training observations. The team wants to standardize every feature and use k-NN. Which handling is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Recognize the zero standard deviation and handle the feature explicitly rather than dividing by zero</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A constant feature has no training variation, so its standardization denominator is zero. It cannot distinguish training neighbors. Remove or otherwise handle it deliberately while considering the application; standardization does not manufacture variation or normality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-24": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>24. Q3-24 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A delivery company groups unlabeled route profiles, predicts delay probability from labeled deliveries, and chooses extra driver staffing under a budget. Which statements correctly describe the pipeline?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Grouping profiles can be an unsupervised descriptive step; B. Estimating delay probability is a supervised predictive step; C. The budget-constrained staffing choice is a prescriptive step</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The response availability and desired output distinguish grouping, prediction, and action. The same domain can support several analytic questions. A model family suitable for one stage need not answer the other stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a></p>"}, "Q3-25": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>25. Q3-25 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which quantities enter the lecture&#x27;s multiplicative Holt-Winters level update?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A level smoothing coefficient alpha; B. Previous level plus previous trend; C. Current observation divided by the previous same-phase seasonal factor</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The update blends a deseasonalized current observation with the previous projected baseline. A future observed response is not available.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-26": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>26. Q3-26 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A linear classifier predicts positive when 2*x1-3*x2+6&gt;0. An observation starts at (0,1); only x2 increases to 3. Which interpretation is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The prediction changes from positive to negative; the score is a decision quantity rather than automatically a probability</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The scores are 3 initially and -3 after the change, so the sign crosses the boundary. The different coefficient signs produce different movement directions. A raw linear classifier score is not bounded as a probability.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q3-27": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>27. Q3-27 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A clinician estimates infection probability at .08. An unnecessary follow-up costs $10; missing an infection costs $200; correct decisions are free. Compare follow-up to no follow-up under these stated costs. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Expected no-follow-up error cost is .08*$200=$16; B. Expected follow-up error cost is .92*$10=$9.20; C. Follow-up has lower stated expected error cost, although this simplified calculation does not include unstated consequences</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Compare the expected consequences of each action, not probability alone. The positive action risks a false-positive cost when infection is absent; the negative action risks a miss when it is present. The result favors follow-up under the explicitly stated simplified costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision consequences and expected cost:</strong> Define the positive outcome, count the two error types, and weight them by their consequences. For one probability-based decision, compare the expected cost of the two actions. Do not assume 50% is universally the right threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a></p>"}, "Q3-28": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>28. Q3-28 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A process records one response per completed part. Each 80-part batch lasts a variable number of days, and early parts repeatedly have lower operating hours before failure. Which modeling interpretations are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. An item-indexed seasonal analysis can use a period of 80; B. A positive trend in this response would indicate increasing operating hours before failure across the index; D. A multiplicative early-batch factor below one corresponds to lower hours before failure</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The index and response definition determine the period and interpretation. A low factor implies shorter observed reliability, while a positive trend implies longer response values. The model describes patterns, not the reason for them.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-29": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>29. Q3-29 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A new customer is assigned to the closest of several stored k-means centers. It is much farther from every center than any training customer was. Which statements are valid?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The nearest-center rule can still assign it to a cluster; D. Its unusual distance warrants checking whether the learned representation covers this case</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The geometric rule returns a nearest center even for an atypical case. Operational similarity and representation coverage need separate judgment. Using learned groups for assignment does not retrospectively add response labels to the original clustering task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-30": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>30. Q3-30 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two infection models have different ROC curves. Model A has a larger AUC; at the clinic&#x27;s chosen operating thresholds, model B has lower expected decision cost. Which conclusions are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Threshold choice and error consequences need consideration beyond AUC; C. The clinic can favor B under its stated operating costs; D. AUC summarizes ranking across positive and negative cases</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> AUC is a useful ranking summary, not a complete value measure for a chosen action rule. Performance at the operational threshold, prevalence, and error costs can favor a different model. This parallels the lecture distinction between accuracy-like measures and decision value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision consequences and expected cost:</strong> Define the positive outcome, count the two error types, and weight them by their consequences. For one probability-based decision, compare the expected cost of the two actions. Do not assume 50% is universally the right threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a></p>"}, "Q3-31": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>31. Q3-31 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The same patient&#x27;s measurements appear in both training and validation, although deployment targets new patients. What is the concern?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Validation can benefit from patient-specific overlap</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Related observations can leak individual patterns across subsets. Grouped splitting by patient better assesses generalization to previously unseen patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-32": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>32. Q3-32 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A time series has roughly stable mean and variability, while another has a strong upward trend. Which statements match the lecture&#x27;s stationarity discussion?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Whether differencing helps should be assessed rather than assumed; C. Differences may make some trending series more stable; D. The stable series better resembles the lecture&#x27;s stationary-data description</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture emphasizes stable temporal behavior and the possibility that differences are more stationary than levels. It does not promise that a particular differencing step cures every series. This is a conceptual suitability check, not a test of formal stationarity theory.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q3-33": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>33. Q3-33 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A level-only smoothing model and Holt give the same current level. The series has an estimated positive trend. Which statements about their ordinary forecasts are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Holt can extend the estimated trend over the forecast horizon; D. Level-only smoothing gives a flat point forecast at its current level</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The model structures differ: level-only smoothing has no explicit trend extrapolation; Holt includes a trend state. Alpha changes responsiveness, not the structure. Forecasting a trend does not explain why it exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q3-34": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>34. Q3-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which are valid reasons for pruning or limiting a tree?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Children would have too few observations; B. A split&#x27;s training improvement does not persist on validation; C. Complexity encourages fitting noise</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Pruning and minimum-size requirements control model flexibility. Tree depth can improve training fit while harming generalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q3-35": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>35. Q3-35 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which circumstances can generate a genuine outlying record?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A one-time publicity event affecting sales; C. A rare weather-related delivery disruption; D. Natural tail variation in a large sample</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The first three can be real outcomes. A known transcription error is a measurement problem, even if the wrongly recorded value looks plausible.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-36": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>36. Q3-36 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Upper CUSUM is reset when its raw cumulative value becomes negative. What purpose does this serve?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. It avoids carrying downward evidence that delays a later upward alarm</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Negative accumulated deviations do not help identify future upward shifts. Resetting improves responsiveness but does not eliminate randomness or false alarms.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-37": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>37. Q3-37 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A forecaster supplies expected returns. A risk manager instead asks how large their fluctuations may be during a volatile period. Which method most directly serves that second target?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. GARCH, because its primary output is time-varying variance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> GARCH targets the conditional variance of an ordered response. A mean forecast, change alarm, cluster assignment, and event probability answer different questions. The selection rests on the requested output rather than the shared financial domain.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q3-38": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>38. Q3-38 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which quantities should remain distinct in a CUSUM analysis?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Baseline expectation mu; B. Per-observation allowance C; D. Cumulative alarm threshold T</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Mu sets the reference level, C discounts small deviations, and T specifies evidence required to alarm. They play different roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-39": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>39. Q3-39 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> When comparing AIC or BIC scores, which precautions matter?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Use comparable observations and likelihood definitions; C. Prefer smaller values for the stated criteria; D. Count parameters consistently</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Likelihood scale and parameter counting affect the comparison. Transformations may require accounting for the changed density scale rather than comparing arbitrary reported numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-40": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>40. Q3-40 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A positive response is transformed with Box-Cox before regression. The fitted model now predicts transformed values. Which reporting practice is defensible at the survey level?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Identify the transformed response and explain how predictions relate to the original units before making operational claims</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Changing the response representation changes the scale of predictions and coefficients. Operational reporting must state that scale and use an appropriate conversion/interpretation. A response transformation does not solve omitted-variable issues or establish causality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Transformations and response scale:</strong> A transformation can improve model behavior but does not guarantee a correct specification. Distinguish predictor transformations, response transformations, and detrending. Report predictions and coefficients on the appropriate scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-41": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>41. Q3-41 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish clustering from classification?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A new point can be assigned to a discovered cluster without known response; C. Clustering discovers groups from feature similarity; D. Classification learns from known response categories</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Clustering groups are algorithmic outputs, not preobserved supervised answers or proven causal types. Their practical meaning must be investigated.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-42": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>42. Q3-42 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which concerns should guide the choice between an interpretable regression and a forest?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Need to explain individual modeled relationships; B. Whether decision-makers can use the output; C. Held-out predictive performance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Model choice depends on intended use and evidence. Complexity and branding do not establish suitability.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q3-43": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>43. Q3-43 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A binary predictor is one for premium members and zero otherwise. In an additive model its coefficient is 7. What is its interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Premium membership is associated with a seven-unit predicted response difference holding other predictors fixed</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A dummy coefficient compares the indicated group with its reference under the model. Interactions can change this comparison across other feature values.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-44": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>44. Q3-44 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about training and validation randomness are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A fitted model can capture accidental training patterns; C. A validation winner can benefit from accidental validation patterns; D. A separate test set reduces selection-related optimism</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Every finite sample contains randomness. Independent testing reduces a particular selection bias but does not make a score exact or deterministic.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-45": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>45. Q3-45 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A response is detrended as r=y-(10+2*t). At t=5 a model predicts r=3. What original-scale response is predicted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. 23</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Restore the fitted trend: 3+(10+2*5)=23. Reporting the residual-scale prediction as the original response would omit the baseline movement.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Transformations and response scale:</strong> A transformation can improve model behavior but does not guarantee a correct specification. Distinguish predictor transformations, response transformations, and detrending. Report predictions and coefficients on the appropriate scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-46": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>46. Q3-46 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A linear SVM score of 2.3 is automatically a predicted probability of 2.3.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> An SVM score is a signed decision function, not automatically a calibrated probability. A probability cannot exceed one.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-47": {"key": "1; one", "html": "<h2>47. Q3-47 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> In six-fold CV, each non-test observation serves as a validation observation ____ time(s).</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 1; one</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each fold is held out once, so every observation is validated once and used to train the other five fits.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q3-48": {"key": "True", "html": "<h2>48. Q3-48 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A larger CUSUM threshold usually requires more accumulated evidence before an alarm.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> True</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Increasing the threshold typically delays detection and reduces false alarms, all other settings equal.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q3-49": {"key": "3", "html": "<h2>49. Q3-49 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The regression residual for observed y=12 and fitted y=9 is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 3</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Residuals conventionally equal observed minus fitted, so 12-9=3.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q3-50": {"key": "80", "html": "<h2>50. Q3-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With actual positive prevalence .1, sensitivity .8, and specificity .9, expected TP per 1,000 observations is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 80</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> There are 100 expected actual positives, and .8 of them are correctly detected, giving 80.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q4-01": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>1. Q4-01 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An analyst says the maximum-likelihood fit chooses parameters that make the observed data most compatible with the assumed statistical model. Another says it chooses parameters that make every future outcome certain. Which statements are valid?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The first description reflects the purpose of maximum-likelihood fitting; D. The likelihood calculation depends on the specified model and assumptions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Likelihood evaluates parameter choices within an assumed model using the observations. It is not a guarantee for new outcomes and can reward extra flexibility. Complexity criteria and validation help assess whether improved fit is useful rather than overfitting.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-02": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>2. Q4-02 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression is fit using two principal components instead of two original, correlated predictors. A manager wants to interpret one fitted component slope as the effect of changing only the first original predictor. What is the best response?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. A component combines original predictors, so its coefficient cannot automatically be read as the effect of one original feature alone</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Component scores are combinations of the original centered/scaled features. Interpretation must account for the loadings and transformation, not rename a component coefficient as a single-feature intervention. PCA changes representation and does not supply causal identification.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-03": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>3. Q4-03 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A PCA transformation has been learned from training predictors. A new production case needs a regression prediction in that component space. Which procedure is appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Apply the stored centering/scaling and loadings, then use the fitted component-space regression</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Training establishes the representation used to fit the downstream model. New cases must be expressed in the same coordinates using stored transformations. Refitting axes or using the unknown response changes the procedure or leaks outcome information.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-04": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>4. Q4-04 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two CUSUM definitions of a hot season give different results. The analyst selects the definition supporting a preferred conclusion, hides the other analysis, and attributes the shift to a policy. Which concerns are warranted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Selecting only a favorable definition can bias the reported conclusion; B. A transparent analysis distinguishes a detected change from its explanation; C. Results should disclose relevant definitions and sensitivity to them</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The measured quantity and chosen baseline/settings affect change detection. Honest reporting acknowledges sensitivity and distinguishes an observed pattern from a cause. A preferred political or operational story does not make selective reporting valid.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-05": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>5. Q4-05 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A hard-margin linear SVM is proposed for labeled observations whose classes overlap in the available predictor space. The team insists that every training observation must lie on the correct side with a nonzero margin. What is the most sensible lecture-level response?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The strict hard-margin requirement may be infeasible; consider a soft-margin tradeoff and validate the resulting classifier</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Hard-margin separation relies on an appropriately separable representation. Encoding labels is not a transformation that separates overlapping classes. Soft-margin modeling permits a fit/margin tradeoff; possible data issues should be investigated rather than inferred from infeasibility alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-06": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>6. Q4-06 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A level-only smoothing model has current estimate 73. A colleague forecasts 73 tomorrow and 83 five observations ahead because the horizon is longer. No trend component has been fitted. Which statement is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The ordinary point forecasts remain 73 at both horizons; a systematic trend extension requires a trend-capable specification</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Level-only smoothing extends the current level as its point forecast. It does not imply zero uncertainty, and the horizon does not create a trend. A trend-capable method such as Holt explicitly supplies that structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-07": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>7. Q4-07 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Default is labeled positive. For the same fixed logistic scores, the bank raises the classification threshold from .2 to .7. Which consequences are generally expected?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The set of positive predictions can only shrink or stay the same; B. False positives can fall, while false negatives can rise</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> For fixed scores, a higher threshold makes the positive rule stricter. It can reduce unnecessary positive actions while missing more true positives. The probabilities do not change merely because the action threshold changes, and value depends on consequences.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a></p>"}, "Q4-08": {"key": "AC", "html": "<h2>8. Q4-08 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two regression predictors move almost together across the data. The fitted model predicts reasonably well, but their individual coefficients change substantially when the sample changes slightly. Which conclusion is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. PCA can be considered as a representation change, but it will not establish causal importance; C. The predictors provide overlapping information, so separate coefficient interpretations can be unstable even when predictions remain useful</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Strongly correlated predictors can make separate effects difficult to estimate and interpret. Prediction and coefficient stability are different questions. PCA may summarize shared variation, while changing interpretation; it is not a causal test.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a></p>"}, "Q4-09": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>9. Q4-09 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A Holt model&#x27;s current trend is positive, while recent observations alternate in a repeatable weekly pattern around that trend. Which model-structure conclusion is reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. A trend component does not by itself model repeating seasonal phases; a seasonal specification may be needed</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Trend captures evolving direction across observations; seasonality captures recurring phase effects. Individual values may move up and down around a positive trend. Changing a smoothing weight does not add missing seasonal states.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-10": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>10. Q4-10 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two equally good candidate methods obtain validation accuracies .81 and .85 due partly to sampling noise. Why may the chosen .85 overstate quality?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Selecting the winner favors favorable validation fluctuations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Even equal true performers can rank differently in a finite sample. Choosing the highest score introduces a winner&#x27;s-selection effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-11": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>11. Q4-11 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A reliability analysis uses multiplicative seasonality. Early-batch items have factor .8, later items 1.2, and the response is hours of operation before first breakdown. Which statements are supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Early-batch response levels are lower relative to the current baseline; C. The seasonal pattern does not by itself determine the cause; D. Higher hours before breakdown correspond to longer observed reliability</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The factor multiplies the baseline response, so .8 corresponds to lower hours and 1.2 to higher hours. The response definition determines the favorable direction. Explanations about workers or equipment require evidence beyond the seasonal pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-12": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>12. Q4-12 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which uses of test data preserve its final evaluation role?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Compute a previously selected evaluation metric; B. Apply a locked preprocessing transformation; D. Apply the already selected model</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Scoring a fixed procedure is legitimate testing. Adapting the procedure after inspecting test outcomes breaks the independence needed for final evaluation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-13": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>13. Q4-13 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An upper CUSUM statistic updates to exactly 10. The written alarm rule is S&gt;T with threshold T=10. A manager treats equality as an alarm and claims a defect cause has been proved. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The allowance and the alarm threshold are different quantities and should not be confused; B. A different rule using S&gt;=T would have different boundary behavior; D. Equality does not trigger this particular strict-inequality rule</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Read the actual comparison: 10 is not greater than 10. Allowance affects increments and threshold affects the decision after accumulation. A change signal, whether triggered now or later, does not identify its cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-14": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>14. Q4-14 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An organization has labeled numeric outcomes and wants to predict a new case from similar historical cases. Three nearest responses are 4, 10, and 13. Which distinction is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. k-NN regression predicts their average, 9; k-NN classification would vote on category labels instead</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The response type determines whether neighbors are averaged or their labels voted. Clustering is a separate unlabeled grouping task. The mean-neighbor estimate is a prediction, not certainty.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a></p>"}, "Q4-15": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>15. Q4-15 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which residual plots suggest different modeling issues?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Long same-sign runs over time can suggest serial dependence; C. Systematic curvature can suggest wrong functional form; D. A funnel can suggest nonconstant error variance</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Diagnostics address conditional errors and structure. Marginal normality of one predictor does not prove linearity, independence, or equal error variance.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Residual plots and regression assumptions:</strong> Separate nonlinearity, unequal residual spread, dependence, and unusually influential observations. A curved pattern suggests a mean-model problem; changing spread suggests a variance problem. Assumptions concern errors conditional on predictors rather than requiring every predictor to be normally distributed.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression diagnostics lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-16": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>16. Q4-16 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A straight-line regression misses a clear curved response pattern. A polynomial term or regression spline improves held-out prediction. Which statements are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. A regression can include nonlinear functions of predictors while remaining linear in its fitted coefficients; D. A spline can join different polynomial pieces smoothly</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Linear in coefficients is different from a straight line in the original feature. Polynomial terms and splines can model curvature in a numeric response. Better prediction does not automatically identify the mechanism generating that pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-17": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>17. Q4-17 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which behaviors are expected within a standard k-means run under its squared-distance objective?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The objective need not reach the global optimum; B. Updating a nonempty cluster to its mean cannot increase its assigned-point SSE; D. Assigning points to closest centers cannot increase WSS</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each alternating step improves or preserves the common objective. Nonincrease within a run does not establish global optimality.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-18": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>18. Q4-18 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team replaces original features with PCA components before regression and retains only a few components. Which statements are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Loadings describe combinations of the original features; C. The component representation may make original-feature interpretation harder; D. Dropping components can discard information useful for predicting the response</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new coordinates. Dimension reduction can remove response-relevant information, and combinations can be harder to interpret than original attributes. Explained predictor variance is not the same metric as explained response variance.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-19": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>19. Q4-19 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A retailer summarizes spending patterns by customer group, predicts next-month spending, and allocates marketing under a fixed budget. Which distinction is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Summary, prediction, and constrained allocation address descriptive, predictive, and prescriptive questions respectively</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The desired output differentiates the analytic tasks. Relationships can inform forecasting and decisions, but a forecast is not an optimization policy and a descriptive relationship is not causal evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q4-20": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>20. Q4-20 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which goals can clustering support in the lectures?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Targeted marketing segments; C. Initial investigation of different subpopulations; D. Geographic facility placement</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Clustering discovers feature-based groupings. Supervised yes/no modeling uses known outcomes and is a different task.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-21": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>21. Q4-21 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements distinguish smoothing from forecasting?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A current smoothed level can use the current observed value; B. Scoring a smoothed value as if it were an advance forecast can be optimistic; D. A forecast for the current value must be produced before observing it</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Information timing matters. Forecast evaluation must compare predictions made without the target observation against its later realization.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-22": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>22. Q4-22 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A model for tomorrow&#x27;s staffing uses tomorrow&#x27;s realized queue length as a predictor and achieves excellent validation error. The decision is made tonight. Which statements are warranted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A valid evaluation should reproduce the information available when staffing is chosen; B. Excellent retrospective error can therefore misrepresent feasible deployment performance; D. The predictor is unavailable at decision time</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A future realized value leaks information unavailable for the real decision. A validation split cannot make that value timely. The predictor set and evaluation timeline must match deployment regardless of model family.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-23": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>23. Q4-23 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which conclusions are supported by a CUSUM alarm?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Accumulated evidence has crossed the chosen threshold; B. A false alarm remains possible; C. The evidence is consistent with a shift relative to the baseline</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A detected statistical change does not prove a mechanism or eliminate the chance of random triggering.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-24": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>24. Q4-24 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A fraud classifier deliberately shifts its decision boundary so more borderline cases are investigated. Missed fraud is expensive, while investigations also consume resources. Which statements follow?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. More true fraud may be caught, but unnecessary investigations can also increase; B. The change should be evaluated using the relevant consequences of both error types; C. Shifting a boundary is a decision tradeoff rather than proof that the probability of fraud changed</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A less restrictive positive rule can trade false negatives for false positives. Whether that improves value depends on both costs and observed performance. Changing the rule does not change the actual event rate or guarantee an optimum.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q4-25": {"key": "ABCD", "html": "<h2>25. Q4-25 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A classification tree has leaves with 18 of 20, 14 of 20, and 1 of 1 training cases classified correctly. A colleague averages the three percentages and uses the resulting high value to prove deployment quality. Which statements identify the errors?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The one-case leaf is a potential overfitting warning; B. Training accuracy should weight leaves by their case counts, giving 33/41; C. Even correctly computed training accuracy is not independent deployment evidence; D. Percentages from differently sized leaves must be weighted by their case counts for overall accuracy</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Add the 33 correct cases and 41 total cases rather than assigning equal weight to very different leaf sizes. The tiny leaf can inflate apparent fit and be unstable. A training score is not a held-out test.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q4-26": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>26. Q4-26 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A medicine-delivery dataset contains real rare delays caused by storms. Which approaches can preserve relevant risk?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Assess how excluding delays changes operational optimism; B. Model disruption probability separately from ordinary delivery duration; C. Retain delays if the target includes such conditions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Rare adverse outcomes may matter greatly to decisions. Their treatment should reflect the target system and loss rather than aesthetic cleanliness.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-27": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>27. Q4-27 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which are legitimate distinctions between ARIMA and GARCH?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. GARCH primarily models conditional variance; B. GARCH uses squared shocks in its variance recursion; C. ARIMA primarily models the conditional level/mean</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> GARCH&#x27;s variance structure has lagged variances and squared innovations, without an ARIMA-style differencing parameter in the lecture formulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Forecasting variability with GARCH:</strong> Distinguish a value or mean forecast from a variance forecast. GARCH uses prior variance and squared errors to represent evolving volatility. Know its output and purpose; detailed fitting mathematics is outside this practice bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q4-28": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>28. Q4-28 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A boxplot flags a delivery delay, and a regression also gives it a large residual. The delay is verified as real and caused serious medicine spoilage. Which handling best matches the target of predicting delivery risk?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Investigate and retain or appropriately represent the real risk rather than deleting it solely to improve fit</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Flags identify unusual observations, not automatically invalid ones. A real extreme event can be central to the intended risk question. A residual may reflect missing context or genuine rarity; it is not proof that the model is complete.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-29": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>29. Q4-29 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A min-max scaler is trained on values from 0 to 10. Which statements about future x=15 are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It lies outside the original training range; B. Min-max fitting did not guarantee bounds for all future observations; D. Its transformed value is 1.5</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The fitted linear map sends 15 to 1.5. Clipping is an additional modeling policy, not an automatic consequence of the scaling formula.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q4-30": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>30. Q4-30 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about k-fold CV follow from its construction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Average scores can compare candidate settings; B. Each fit excludes the held-out fold&#x27;s outcomes from training; D. Every fold is held out in turn</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> CV is a resampling evaluation procedure. It does not mandate using the intermediate fits as an ensemble.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-31": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>31. Q4-31 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A forest&#x27;s importance chart ranks a predictor highly. A manager says increasing that predictor must increase the response by a fixed amount and cause an improvement. Which correction is most appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Importance indicates predictive contribution under that fitted procedure; it need not give a signed linear effect or establish a causal intervention effect</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A contribution ranking is not a signed constant slope. Trees can use different relationships in different regions, and correlated information can complicate interpretation. Predictive contribution does not establish a feasible or causal intervention.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests and diverse trees:</strong> Know how randomized predictor choices can diversify supervised trees and why combining different strengths may help. Averaging or voting does not repair leakage or prove causality. Bootstrap/OOB calculations are not required for this bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-32": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>32. Q4-32 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about Box-Cox and regression assumptions are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Lambda is a transformation parameter; B. Normality relevant to conventional regression inference concerns errors conditional on predictors; D. The standard Box-Cox family requires positive input values</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Transformation choice and residual diagnostics are separate from raw marginal appearance. A normal-looking response histogram does not prove conditional-error assumptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Transformations and response scale:</strong> A transformation can improve model behavior but does not guarantee a correct specification. Distinguish predictor transformations, response transformations, and detrending. Report predictions and coefficients on the appropriate scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-33": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>33. Q4-33 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team changes income units from dollars to thousands of dollars. It keeps an unscaled k-NN distance rule otherwise unchanged and compares it with a tree using equivalent rescaled thresholds. Which statements are reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. An equivalent monotone rescaling preserves the ordering relevant to a tree threshold; C. The change can alter the relative weight of income in unscaled neighbor distances; D. The transformation should be applied consistently to training and new observations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Distance geometry depends on numerical scales. Tree threshold partitions can be preserved by an equivalent monotone unit change. The sensitivity differs between methods, and all future cases must use the same representation as training.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-34": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>34. Q4-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about ordinary trees and model trees are accurate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Leaf-specific regressions require enough observations to estimate coefficients; B. A model tree can fit a separate regression within a leaf; C. Ordinary regression CART often uses a constant leaf mean</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture contrasts conceptual leaf regressions with practical simple leaf predictions. They are distinct model variants.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q4-35": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>35. Q4-35 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For overlapping training classes, which statements about soft-margin SVM are reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Some violations can be accepted; B. Class-specific loss multipliers can reflect asymmetric costs; C. Violations are balanced against margin regularization</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Soft-margin formulations allow loss rather than requiring perfect hard separation. Overlap need not make the optimization infeasible.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-36": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>36. Q4-36 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A dataset includes ordinary and holiday operations. A random split happens to place nearly all holiday cases in training, while the model will be deployed on both types. Which conclusion is supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The validation set may inadequately represent deployment, so its score should be interpreted with that coverage concern</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Randomness does not guarantee subgroup coverage in a finite sample. Validation needs to represent the intended use. More model complexity cannot replace evaluation cases from relevant operating conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-37": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>37. Q4-37 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A forecast team has only a short, noisy demand history and asks whether an ARIMA model is automatically preferable to exponential smoothing because it is more general. Which response best reflects the survey lecture?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Generality does not ensure a better forecast; data amount, behavior, and validation should guide the comparison</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture presents ARIMA as a broader family, with data requirements and behavior affecting suitability. Flexible specifications still require adequate information and evaluation. GARCH targets variance rather than replacing a demand-value model.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-38": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>38. Q4-38 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which choices must be distinguished when specifying Holt-Winters?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Cycle length in observations; B. Additive versus multiplicative seasonal form; D. Observation frequency</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Frequency determines the units of L, and seasonal form determines whether effects are offsets or proportions. Response categories are not a seasonal cycle setting.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-39": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>39. Q4-39 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A call-center model predicts the number of arrivals in an interval from time of day and holiday status. Which introduced regression family naturally models this count mean?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Poisson regression</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Poisson regression relates a nonnegative count mean to predictors. Its distributional assumptions and exposure interval still need checking.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q4-40": {"key": "AC", "html": "<h2>40. Q4-40 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which conclusions are justified by ROC AUC=.9?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The scores have strong positive-versus-negative ranking under the evaluation distribution; C. It specifies neither a deployment threshold nor its cost</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> AUC summarizes pairwise ranking over thresholds. It does not determine probability calibration, any single confusion matrix, or economic value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q4-41": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>41. Q4-41 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A k-means segmentation uses distance squared=4*(income difference squared)+(age difference squared) after representing both features on agreed scales. A manager calls the weights learned regression effects. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The weights define similarity, not coefficients of a supervised response model; D. The stated weighting makes income differences contribute more to this distance than equal-sized age differences</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Distance weights define geometry in feature space. They can change assignments without expressing a regression relationship or causal effect. Their appropriateness depends on meaningful similarity for the segmentation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q4-42": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>42. Q4-42 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about k-NN model behavior are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its distance definition can change the selected neighbors; C. It stores/usefully retains labeled examples for prediction; D. Increasing k may smooth local variation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> k-NN classifies through local examples rather than fitting an SVM hyperplane. Its geometry and neighborhood size are substantive choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-43": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>43. Q4-43 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two candidate models have similar fit on the same observations. BIC prefers the simpler one more strongly than AIC does. Which interpretation is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The criteria use different complexity penalties; disagreement does not require a computation error</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Both criteria trade likelihood against complexity, using different penalties. They can favor different specifications or express different strengths of preference. Neither is a classification accuracy measure or proof of causal truth.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-44": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>44. Q4-44 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team has 12 historical observations but wants an ARIMA specification with many value and error lags. It treats the lecture&#x27;s approximate 40-observation guidance as an absolute law. Which statements are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. A simpler model and realistic validation may be sensible alternatives; C. The guidance is a rule of thumb rather than a universal guarantee or prohibition; D. The very short history and flexible specification raise a legitimate estimation and overfitting concern</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Sample guidance provides context, not a magic threshold. Many lagged terms with little data can be unstable. More observations help without guaranteeing adequacy; model structure and validation remain relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q4-45": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>45. Q4-45 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A cluster-based service plan assigns a remote new demand point to the least-distant existing center. The manager concludes the current facility can serve it within the required response time. What is the best correction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Nearest among existing centers does not imply close enough for the service standard; coverage must be evaluated separately</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Assignment answers which center is nearest, not whether the distance satisfies a service requirement. The operational limit and distance representation need to be checked. A grouping result does not itself provide complete feasibility evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-46": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>46. Q4-46 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Permuting the names of clusters changes WSS when centers and memberships stay the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cluster identifiers are arbitrary. Relabeling the same partition does not change distances or fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-47": {"key": "4", "html": "<h2>47. Q4-47 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> For upper CUSUM with previous statistic=0, observation=18, baseline=12, and allowance=2, the new statistic is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 4</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> max(0,0+18-12-2)=4.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q4-48": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>48. Q4-48 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A seasonal multiplicative factor below one necessarily reveals why that seasonal pattern occurs.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> It describes a proportional decrease in response at that phase. It does not identify the causal mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q4-49": {"key": "0.5; 50%", "html": "<h2>49. Q4-49 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If PCA eigenvalues are 5,3,2, the first component&#x27;s predictor-variance fraction is ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.5; 50%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Divide its eigenvalue by the total: 5/(5+3+2)=.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q4-50": {"key": "0.8; 80%", "html": "<h2>50. Q4-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> With TP=20,FN=5,FP=15,TN=60, specificity equals ____.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> 0.8; 80%</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Specificity conditions on actual negatives: 60/(60+15)=.8.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q5-01": {"key": "AB", "html": "<h2>1. Q5-01 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team clusters unlabeled customers and also fits a repayment classifier using known outcomes. It proposes using PCA learned on the full dataset before evaluating either procedure. Which statements distinguish the tasks and evaluation issue?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A learned PCA representation intended for predictive evaluation should be fitted within the training procedure; B. Clustering discovers feature-based groups, while classification learns from outcome labels</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Unsupervised grouping and supervised prediction use different information. A representation learned from all observations can still contaminate an assessment of a procedure meant to learn from training data. PCA does not attach outcome meanings to cluster IDs.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a></p>"}, "Q5-02": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>2. Q5-02 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A verified demand spike came from a unique publicity event. A team wants forecasts for ordinary weeks, while another team wants emergency capacity planning that includes rare surges. Which handling is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The ordinary-week model&#x27;s error cannot be presented as performance for all weeks without qualification; B. Its removal can be justified only by checking validity and target relevance, not by a better fitted score alone; D. The event&#x27;s relevance can differ between the two target questions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The same real observation can be outside one restricted target and crucial to another. Exclusion must be tied to the intended population and reported transparently. Better ordinary-week fit does not establish performance during rare surges.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-03": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>3. Q5-03 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which questions should precede outlier handling?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. What generated its unusual value?; C. Is the record valid?; D. Must the intended predictions or decisions cover that circumstance?</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Validity, origin, and relevance guide treatment. Optimizing a cosmetic fit metric alone can hide important system behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-04": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>4. Q5-04 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression with many fitted parameters looks excellent on a small dataset. A corrected information criterion penalizes it more than uncorrected AIC. Which interpretation is appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. The correction addresses the finite-data situation and can discourage over-complex fitting</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Corrected AIC adjusts the tradeoff for finite samples rather than changing the observations. A small dataset can support less complexity than its training fit suggests. Information criteria guide comparison without replacing assumptions and independent evaluation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-05": {"key": "AC", "html": "<h2>5. Q5-05 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A hospital groups patient pickup locations with k-means to propose transport hubs. Some routes require crossing a river at a bridge. It then uses a classifier to predict whether each patient needs urgent pickup. Which statements separate the roles correctly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The classifier needs known urgency outcomes or labels for training; C. Clustering can suggest geographic groups, but route constraints still matter to hub placement</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Grouping location features does not infer urgency labels. Supervised urgency prediction and operational transport planning require different information. Barriers affect whether geographic distances represent feasible service.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-06": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>6. Q5-06 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements about a cluster-based facility plan are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The number of facilities can constrain k; C. A center is an analytical candidate, not automatically a feasible building site; D. Coverage and travel constraints may require additional analysis</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Clustering supports planning but may not directly encode budgets, allowed sites, travel networks, or coverage limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-07": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>7. Q5-07 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A regression tree considers a split into a two-case child and an eighteen-case child. The two-case child has no training variation, and total training error falls slightly. Which reasoning best follows the lecture?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. Assess improvement and adequate child support, then validate or prune; the tiny perfect leaf is not sufficient evidence of usefulness</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Fit improvement must be interpreted with leaf support and held-out evidence. A tiny leaf can isolate noise. Overall fit accounts for the observations, and pruning helps reject training-only benefits.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q5-08": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>8. Q5-08 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Reliability is measured as operating hours before failure for each successive manufactured item; production batches contain 150 items. Which interpretations are supported?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. A seasonal factor .7 at a phase represents a lower proportional response; B. Cycle length can be 150 observations; D. A positive trend means modeled reliability response rises over successive items</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Index units and response meaning determine interpretation. Trend and seasonal patterns describe associations, not mechanisms by themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-09": {"key": "BC", "html": "<h2>9. Q5-09 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two standardized predictor columns are exact duplicates. PCA is used before regression. Which statements are reasonable?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Keeping the first component can summarize the shared predictor information; C. The second direction has no additional variation beyond the first</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Perfect redundancy creates only one varying direction in this predictor pair. A component can summarize that information, but response predictability still depends on the relation to the response. Duplicate measurements do not independently identify causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q5-10": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>10. Q5-10 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Eight-fold validation on 1,600 non-test observations selects a regression specification. The final test set has not been used. Which fitting plan reflects the lecture approach?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Refit the chosen specification on all 1,600 non-test observations and then evaluate once on the untouched test set</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Cross-validation is a selection procedure. The specification can then use all non-test observations to estimate its final parameters. The test set remains separate from further selection; fold coefficients do not define the required final fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q5-11": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>11. Q5-11 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which features make a modeling conclusion useful and defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Its limitations and assumptions are understood; B. It answers the actual operational question; C. It respects the information and population represented by the data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lectures emphasize matching models to real situations and interpreting output honestly. Agreement with prior beliefs is not a validation criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q5-12": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>12. Q5-12 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> The team compares SVM, k-NN, and a tree on a training/validation split. Before splitting, it selects features by checking their relationships with all response labels. Which statements identify the concern?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The validation outcomes influenced feature selection; B. Using several model families does not repair that leakage; D. Feature selection should be part of the training and validation procedure rather than use the held-out labels beforehand</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The fitted coefficients are only one learned part of the pipeline. Response-driven feature selection also uses outcome information. The validation set was already involved, regardless of which model family is subsequently fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q5-13": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>13. Q5-13 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A volatility model reports high variance after a quiet latest observation because earlier volatility was high. A manager says zero latest signed error must force zero variance. Which correction fits GARCH&#x27;s lecture-level mechanism?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Lagged variance as well as squared errors can carry volatility forward; a quiet new observation need not erase it</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The GARCH structure uses prior variance and squared errors. Volatility can persist even without a large latest shock. Variance does not express a return direction, and the lecture distinguishes its lag structure from ARIMA differencing.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Forecasting variability with GARCH:</strong> Distinguish a value or mean forecast from a variance forecast. GARCH uses prior variance and squared errors to represent evolving volatility. Know its output and purpose; detailed fitting mathematics is outside this practice bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://arch.readthedocs.io/en/latest/univariate/introduction.html\">arch project: mean and volatility model components</a></p>"}, "Q5-14": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>14. Q5-14 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A multiplicative seasonal factor is estimated at 1.2 for the current phase of a production cycle. The team instead proposes an additive seasonal term of +1.2 in the same response units without refitting. Which statement is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The two quantities mean different things: a multiplicative factor rescales the baseline, while an additive effect is an amount on the response scale</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A ratio around one and an amount around zero are different representations of seasonality. They are not interchangeable without considering the response scale and model. Neither is a guarantee about individual observations or an explanation of cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-15": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>15. Q5-15 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Monthly output increases roughly linearly, while its first differences appear much more stable. A team proposes differencing and then an ARIMA model; another proposes a trend component in smoothing. Which conclusions are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The resulting candidate forecasts should be compared rather than declaring one universally best; D. Differencing and explicit trend smoothing are different ways of representing temporal structure</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The approaches handle temporal structure differently. A plausible representation motivates a candidate but does not guarantee performance. Differencing order is a modeling choice to assess, and observed trends do not identify causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-16": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>16. Q5-16 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> At the same k, two k-means starts produce WSS=90 and WSS=100. For a larger k, another run produces WSS=60. Which comparisons are justified?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Different starts can lead to different local solutions; D. At the same k, the lower-WSS run is better under the stated training objective</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Repeated starts compare solutions to the same objective and k. Comparing different k values also changes flexibility, so a lower objective does not resolve usefulness. K-means can stop at different local solutions, which motivates repeated starts.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-17": {"key": "C", "html": "<h2>17. Q5-17 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A calibrated probability model predicts p for a rare fault. False alarms cost $3, missed faults cost $9, and correct actions cost zero. At p=.30, which action has lower stated expected error cost?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Declare a fault: expected cost .70*3=$2.10 is less than .30*9=$2.70</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The positive action incurs false-alarm cost when the fault is absent; the negative action incurs missed-fault cost when it is present. Comparing those explicit expected costs favors the positive action here. Probability alone or a universal 50% rule does not determine value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision consequences and expected cost:</strong> Define the positive outcome, count the two error types, and weight them by their consequences. For one probability-based decision, compare the expected cost of the two actions. Do not assume 50% is universally the right threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a></p>"}, "Q5-18": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>18. Q5-18 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two candidate neighbor rules have validation costs $12 and $8 per 100 decisions despite accuracies .95 and .90 respectively. If minimizing this measured cost is the objective, which is favored?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The .90-accuracy rule with $8 cost</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The chosen objective is decision cost. Different error types can make a lower-accuracy model more valuable.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision consequences and expected cost:</strong> Define the positive outcome, count the two error types, and weight them by their consequences. For one probability-based decision, compare the expected cost of the two actions. Do not assume 50% is universally the right threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-19": {"key": "AD", "html": "<h2>19. Q5-19 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A manufacturer records item position, production day, and operating hours before failure across fixed 150-item batches. Item position and production day are highly correlated. It wants to examine early-batch reliability. Which statements align the time-series and PCA roles?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. PCA of the two correlated predictors can summarize their shared variation before a factor-based regression; D. An item-indexed seasonal model uses reliability hours as the response and 150 as the cycle length</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The seasonal period is measured in index steps, while the response supplies the interpreted outcome. PCA summarizes predictor variation and may support a separate regression representation. Neither the seasonal pattern nor PCA explains a failure mechanism by itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-20": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>20. Q5-20 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An upper CUSUM starts at zero with baseline 10 and allowance 1. Three readings of 12 arrive. The threshold is 2 and the alarm rule is S&gt;2. Which interpretation is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The statistics are 1, 2, and 3; the first alarm occurs on the third reading under this rule</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Each reading adds 12-10-1=1. Strictly exceeding two occurs at the third update. This is the point of accumulation: modest persistent deviations can build evidence. The signal does not identify a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-21": {"key": "BD", "html": "<h2>21. Q5-21 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> An analyst has many correlated numerical measurements and proposes keeping a few leading PCA components before fitting a predictive model. Which statements fit the survey-level purpose and limits?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The downstream model should be evaluated because PCA did not use the response to define its directions; D. PCA can reduce the number of coordinates while preserving much of the measured predictor variation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Leading components summarize predictor variance and can simplify a representation. Retention is not a guarantee about response signal. Predictive usefulness is assessed in the complete procedure, not deduced solely from the variance fraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a></p>"}, "Q5-22": {"key": "CD", "html": "<h2>22. Q5-22 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A response is usually positive but sometimes zero. The team plans multiplicative Holt-Winters updates that divide by a seasonal factor and current level. Which choices are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> C. Consider whether a different seasonal representation better matches the response behavior; D. Check whether the model&#x27;s divisions and scale assumptions are suitable for these observations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The multiplicative equations require suitable denominators and a meaningful relative seasonal effect. Zero and near-zero behavior can make that formulation problematic. The representation must be checked, rather than blindly applying or relabeling it.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt-Winters seasonality and indexing:</strong> Use the observation index to define the seasonal period: a manufacturing batch can supply the cycle even if its calendar duration varies. Multiplicative factors are ratios around one; additive effects are amounts around zero. Match the forecast to the correct future seasonal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt-winters.html\">Forecasting textbook: seasonal smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-23": {"key": "AC", "html": "<h2>23. Q5-23 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A random forest outperforms one tree on validation, but both models were trained with a predictor recorded after the decision deadline. Which conclusions follow?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Tree diversity does not repair unavailable-predictor leakage; C. The candidate pipeline must be revised and reassessed using timely predictors</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Ensembling can improve predictions under a given representation, but it cannot make later information available earlier. Fix the prediction-time input set and reevaluate the procedure. A relative gain within a flawed setup is not proof of real-world usability.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Random forests and diverse trees:</strong> Know how randomized predictor choices can diversify supervised trees and why combining different strengths may help. Averaging or voting does not repair leakage or prove causality. Bootstrap/OOB calculations are not required for this bank.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/J4Wdy0Wc_xQ\">StatQuest random-forest video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q5-24": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>24. Q5-24 — M9</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which statements correctly contrast feature selection and feature extraction?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Extraction can reduce dimension without selecting original columns directly; C. Selection retains a subset of original variables; D. PCA extraction constructs linear combinations</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A component typically combines multiple features. Selection and extraction can both reduce dimensionality through different representations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — PCA purpose, components, and limitations:</strong> PCA summarizes predictor variation using new component directions without response labels. Correlated predictors can yield a dominant direction. Retaining much predictor variance does not guarantee response prediction or original-feature interpretability.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/FgakZw6K1QQ\">StatQuest PCA video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-25": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>25. Q5-25 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A clinic has binary infection labels and symptoms available at screening. It wants probabilities for risk communication, class decisions for triage, and an alarm if average symptom severity shifts over time. Which statements assign roles correctly?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Logistic regression directly addresses infection probability from symptoms; B. A class decision can use a chosen threshold on that probability; C. CUSUM of ordered symptom measurements addresses the shift-detection request</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Probability prediction, triage, and process monitoring are different outputs. Logistic regression uses labeled binary outcomes; a decision threshold uses its output; CUSUM monitors ordered deviations. Unlabeled groups do not automatically have infection-probability meanings.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/yIYKR4sgzI8\">StatQuest logistic-regression video</a></p>"}, "Q5-26": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>26. Q5-26 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which contrasts between time-series methods are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Simple smoothing extrapolates a level without explicit trend; B. Holt-Winters adds a repeating seasonal component; D. Holt adds an evolving trend</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The smoothing variants progressively add components. GARCH models conditional variance rather than grouping observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Single exponential smoothing and flat forecasts:</strong> Calculate a level update and expand two steps to see declining historical weights. Alpha governs the response to new data. Without trend or seasonality, future point forecasts all equal the latest level; do not keep updating with unknown future observations.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html\">Forecasting textbook: single smoothing</a></p>"}, "Q5-27": {"key": "B", "html": "<h2>27. Q5-27 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A linear classifier predicts positive for x1+x2&gt;6. A case moves from (2,3) to (2,5). A manager attributes the resulting class change to a causal effect of increasing x2. Which interpretation is correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The predicted class changes because the case crosses the rule, but that mathematical change is not proof of a causal effect in the real system</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The sums are five and seven, so the fitted rule changes its decision. A prediction rule describes what the model outputs at those coordinates; it does not establish what a real-world intervention will cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a></p>"}, "Q5-28": {"key": "A", "html": "<h2>28. Q5-28 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A production regression finds an apparently useful negative association between speed and reliability. Its interval is wide and includes zero, and speed is correlated with batch conditions. Which reporting approach is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Report the estimated direction and uncertainty, and avoid a causal recommendation unsupported by the design</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The estimate is a conditional association with uncertainty. An interval including zero is not proof of no relationship, and correlated conditions can complicate attribution. A causal operating recommendation needs more evidence than the coefficient sign.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Regression uncertainty, association, and practical value:</strong> Interpret coefficients conditional on the included predictors, with units and uncertainty. Significance does not establish causality or practical importance; an interval including zero does not prove no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a></p>"}, "Q5-29": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>29. Q5-29 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> If class prevalence changes but sensitivity and specificity stay fixed, which metrics generally can change?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Overall accuracy; B. Precision; C. Expected cost per observation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Precision, accuracy, and cost mix performance with class frequencies. Sensitivity is conditioned on actual positives and is held fixed in the question.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q5-30": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>30. Q5-30 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A safety monitor is retuned to alarm with less accumulated evidence. Missed developing faults are especially costly, but false alarms interrupt production. Which statements describe the tradeoff?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. The threshold should be evaluated against both consequences; C. A lower threshold can detect shifts sooner; D. A lower threshold can also increase false alarms</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Reducing the evidence required can improve responsiveness while sacrificing false-alarm control. The preferred setting depends on the application&#x27;s consequences and evidence, not a guarantee of eliminating both kinds of failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-31": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>31. Q5-31 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A forecast model has an estimated rising trend, but a control engineer needs to flag a sudden increase relative to a stable reference. Which statements correctly separate the two goals?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. CUSUM directly accumulates evidence relative to the stated reference; C. Holt can model level plus an evolving trend for forecasting; D. The engineer should choose a baseline meaningful for the monitoring task rather than treating a forecasting output as the same decision</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Trend extrapolation and alarm generation have different roles. A monitoring baseline and threshold define evidence for a change signal; a forecast state is not automatically an alarm rule or a cause. The task determines what output is needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Holt level, trend, and forecast horizon:</strong> Keep level and trend as different quantities. Update level using the prior projected level and update trend using the level change. An h-step ordinary Holt forecast is level+h*trend. Trend units are response units per observation interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/holt.html\">Forecasting textbook: trend smoothing</a> · <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-32": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>32. Q5-32 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which are distinct choices in a k-NN classification specification?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Distance metric; C. Number of neighbors; D. Feature scaling/weights</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The first three define neighborhoods and their size. Innovation lags belong to time-series models, not ordinary k-NN.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-33": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>33. Q5-33 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A cancer-screening classifier evaluates 1,000 people and produces TP=8, FN=2, FP=90, TN=900. Which statements are correct?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The rare positive class makes accuracy alone an incomplete summary for this screening task; C. Accuracy is (8+900)/1000=.908; D. Among positive predictions, 8/98 actually have cancer</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Accuracy is 90.8%, while the positive-result composition uses TP+FP=98. A large majority of negatives can make accuracy look strong even with important positive-class errors. Costs and the task-specific denominators remain necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Confusion metrics, prevalence, and ROC:</strong> Define the positive event before writing TP,FP,FN,TN. Recall conditions on actual positives, precision on predicted positives, and specificity on actual negatives. ROC plots TPR versus FPR across thresholds. AUC measures ranking; it does not prescribe a threshold or guarantee probability calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q5-34": {"key": "ABCD", "html": "<h2>34. Q5-34 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A clinic expects disease prevalence .20. A model has sensitivity .75 and specificity .90. Missing disease costs $20, an unnecessary follow-up costs $1, and correct decisions are free. Which statements correctly translate this into expected error cost?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Prevalence, both class-conditional error rates, and costs are needed for this population comparison; B. The population false-negative fraction is .20*(1-.75)=.05; C. The expected error cost per person is .05*20+.08*1=$1.08; D. The population false-positive fraction is .80*(1-.90)=.08</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Class-conditional error rates must be weighted by the prevalence of the relevant actual class. The costs then weight those population fractions. Sensitivity describes only the diseased group and cannot determine the whole cost by itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Decision consequences and expected cost:</strong> Define the positive outcome, count the two error types, and weight them by their consequences. For one probability-based decision, compare the expected cost of the two actions. Do not assume 50% is universally the right threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification and decision-rule lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4jRBRDbJemM\">StatQuest ROC video</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: confusion metrics</a></p>"}, "Q5-35": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>35. Q5-35 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which can explain a correlation without a direct causal effect of x on y?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Reverse causation; C. Selection or omitted-variable structure; D. A common cause affects both</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Statistical association can arise through several pathways. A significance test alone cannot establish an intervention effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Regression uncertainty, association, and practical value:</strong> Interpret coefficients conditional on the included predictors, with units and uncertainty. Significance does not establish causality or practical importance; an interval including zero does not prove no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/vemZtEM63GY\">StatQuest p-value video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR regression-inference lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-36": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>36. Q5-36 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A restaurant estimates arrival counts from time of day and holiday status, groups menu demand patterns, and decides purchase quantities under spoilage costs. Which statements assign methods or roles appropriately?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. K-means can group feature profiles without a labeled outcome; C. Purchase decisions require the operational objective in addition to the predictive inputs; D. Poisson regression can model an attribute-dependent arrival count rate</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Poisson regression and clustering target different outputs; both may inform an operation. Inventory action depends on costs and constraints beyond a count forecast. The survey distinction is about roles, not a full optimization derivation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a> · <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a></p>"}, "Q5-37": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>37. Q5-37 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can a single holdout estimate vary materially across random splits?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Each fit may change when its training subset changes; B. Different splits allocate rare or influential cases differently; C. Finite validation sets contain random variation</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Both fitted models and evaluation samples change across splits. Resampling can make selection more stable but cannot remove all uncertainty.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-38": {"key": "ABCD", "html": "<h2>38. Q5-38 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Four teams need a count rate from attributes, a smoothly varying numeric response, updated coefficient beliefs with few observations, and a numeric prediction from similar labeled records. Which pairings match the introduced methods?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Regression splines can join polynomial pieces with smooth connections; B. Poisson regression can address the attribute-dependent count rate; C. k-NN regression averages nearby numeric responses without requiring one global straight-line equation; D. Bayesian regression can combine starting coefficient distributions with observed data</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> These are purpose/mechanism distinctions from the advanced-method survey. Poisson models a rate/mean from attributes; splines join pieces; Bayesian regression updates prior information; neighbor regression averages nearby observed responses without requiring a global linear equation.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}, "Q5-39": {"key": "ABD", "html": "<h2>39. Q5-39 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A trained model is sensitive to a unit change from dollars to thousands of dollars. Which explanations can be relevant?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. An unadjusted distance metric changes its numerical geometry; B. An unadjusted norm penalty can change its relative feature treatment; D. Consistent preprocessing can address the scale change</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Distance- and penalty-based methods depend on representation. A consistent scale policy helps avoid arbitrary unit effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Scaling, units, and distance:</strong> Work through z=(x-mean)/SD and min-max scaling. Explain why changing units changes an unscaled distance. Learn transformation parameters on training data, then reuse them. Distinguish feature standardization from making each observation a unit-length vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html\">scikit-learn preprocessing reference</a></p>"}, "Q5-40": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>40. Q5-40 — M6</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which choices could reasonably improve the interpretation of a CUSUM study?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Check sensitivity to plausible C and T values; C. Report a narrow conclusion matching the measured outcome; D. Use a baseline appropriate to the unchanged regime</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Baseline, settings, and scope affect the inference. Change detection identifies evidence relative to a model, not arbitrary broad causal claims.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — CUSUM direction, allowance, and alarm threshold:</strong> Trace Splus=max(0,Splus_previous+x-mu-C). C is an allowance subtracted from each increment; T is a separate alarm threshold. A lower monitor reverses the deviation. An alarm signals a change under the chosen baseline and settings; it does not establish a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc323.htm\">NIST CUSUM explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-41": {"key": "ACD", "html": "<h2>41. Q5-41 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A team models log(cost) instead of cost to improve a regression&#x27;s behavior. The manager interprets an added unit of the transformed-response prediction as an added dollar of expected cost. Which concerns are warranted?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. The transformed response and raw-dollar response have different scales; C. Predictive performance should be judged on a scale relevant to the application, not mislabeled units; D. Reporting should explain how the fitted predictions relate to the original units</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Log transformation changes the quantity being predicted and the meaning of model output. A raw-dollar statement requires an appropriate interpretation/conversion; a transformed-unit increase is not automatically one dollar. At this survey level, the key is scale awareness rather than a detailed distributional correction.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Linear regression, coefficient interpretation, and interactions:</strong> Linear refers to coefficients, so powers and interactions of predictors can appear in a linear model. Hold other predictors fixed when interpreting an additive coefficient. With interactions, differentiate or compare complete predictions because the effect depends on the interacting predictor.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-regression lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: multiple regression</a> · <a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc52.htm\">NIST Box-Cox explanation</a></p>"}, "Q5-42": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>42. Q5-42 — M3</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which claims about a final test score are appropriate?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. It may fail to represent deployment after a substantial distribution shift; B. It still has finite-sample uncertainty; C. It estimates performance under the test sampling conditions</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Independent testing assesses the selected method for a represented population, not all possible environments. Sample variability and changing conditions remain relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Cross-validation, leakage, and selection optimism:</strong> Draw the outer test holdout and inner CV folds. Repeat learned preprocessing inside each training fold. After selecting settings, refit on the non-test data and evaluate once on the untouched test set. Group or order observations when deployment requires it.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fSytzGwwBVw\">StatQuest cross-validation video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR resampling lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-43": {"key": "ABC", "html": "<h2>43. Q5-43 — M4</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which interpretations of a large optimal WSS decrease are defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> A. Adding a cluster improves feature-space fit; B. The new group may merit substantive investigation; C. Operational relevance can differ from mathematical improvement</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Unsupervised objective improvement is not proof of supervised labels or usefulness for a particular decision. Investigate what the group represents.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — K-means, distance geometry, and elbow selection:</strong> Sketch assign-to-nearest-center and recompute-means steps. For standard k-means, centers minimize squared Euclidean distance within assigned groups. WSS rewards additional clusters, so minimum WSS alone cannot determine a useful k. Compare geometry to the actual travel or service objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/4b5d3muPQmA\">StatQuest k-means video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-44": {"key": "BCD", "html": "<h2>44. Q5-44 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A clinic has labeled symptom records and needs an infection-positive or infection-negative class prediction for new patients. Which lecture approaches can be used for this classification task?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> B. Classification trees; C. k-NN classification; D. SVM</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> SVM, neighbors, and trees directly use known response labels. Clustering discovers feature groups without those labels.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Nearest neighbors and the meaning of k:</strong> Compare classification voting with regression averaging. Specify k, distance, scaling, weights, and tie handling. A larger neighborhood smooths the prediction but can hide local patterns; choose settings using held-out performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: nearest neighbors</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR statistical-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-45": {"key": "D", "html": "<h2>45. Q5-45 — M8</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A model with lower AIC predicts slightly worse on representative held-out cases than an alternative. Management&#x27;s goal is the held-out decision cost, and the lower-AIC model also has higher cost. Which conclusion is defensible?</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> D. AIC is useful model-comparison evidence, but it does not override the stated operational objective and appropriate validation evidence</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> An information criterion evaluates fit and complexity, not arbitrary business consequences. Compare the whole deployed procedure against the stated objective with suitable evidence. Different comparison measures need not agree, and none replaces the definition of value.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Likelihood and complexity criteria:</strong> Likelihood describes fit under assumptions. AIC, corrected AIC, and BIC balance fit and complexity in different ways; lower comparable scores are preferred. A criterion does not guarantee the best operational decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR model-selection lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-46": {"key": "True", "html": "<h2>46. Q5-46 — M1</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A model family, a predictor specification, and a fitted numerical equation can all be called a model in these lectures.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> True</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> The lecture explicitly uses model at these three levels of detail.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Model purpose and interpretation:</strong> Before choosing a method, name the response, available predictors, intended decision, and deployment population. Separate explanation of past observations, prediction of future outcomes, and optimization of actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR author companion courses</a></p>"}, "Q5-47": {"key": "validation; held-out", "html": "<h2>47. Q5-47 — M2</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> Two soft-margin SVM settings fit training data differently. To select the setting for prediction, compare their ____ performance rather than training fit alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> validation; held-out</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A penalty setting is a model-selection choice. Compare held-out validation performance under the intended objective, then assess the selected procedure on independent final test data.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — SVM boundaries, margin, and fit tradeoffs:</strong> Identify which features affect the fitted boundary. Distinguish hard separation from a soft-margin tradeoff, and inspect the stated penalty role. Use validation and the relevant error costs; optimization derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/efR1C6CvhmE\">StatQuest SVM video</a> · <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR classification lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-48": {"key": "False", "html": "<h2>48. Q5-48 — M5</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A collective outlier must contain at least one individual value that is extreme without any context.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> False</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> A sequence can be abnormal collectively while each individual value is plausible. Missing expected events are a lecture example.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Outliers, context, and legitimate rare events:</strong> Ask whether a flagged point is an error, an unusual but relevant case, a context-specific anomaly, or part of an unusual sequence. Exclusion needs a reason connected to the intended population and task. Compare the consequences of preserving and excluding the observation.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR linear-model diagnostics and unsupervised-learning lectures</a></p>"}, "Q5-49": {"key": "autoregressive/value; innovation/error", "html": "<h2>49. Q5-49 — M7</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> ARIMA(p,d,q) uses p for ____ lags and q for ____ lags.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> autoregressive/value; innovation/error</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> p counts lagged transformed values and q lagged innovations, while d is the differencing order.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — ARIMA: AR lags, differencing, and MA innovations:</strong> Read p,d,q in order. AR terms use previous values of the modeled series; d specifies differencing; MA terms use previous innovations. This MA component is different from averaging recent observed values. Check whether the differenced series has an adequately stable mean and dependence structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/non-seasonal-arima.html\">Forecasting textbook: ARIMA</a> · <a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/stationarity.html\">Forecasting textbook: stationarity and differencing</a></p>"}, "Q5-50": {"key": "mean; arithmetic mean; average", "html": "<h2>50. Q5-50 — M10</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> A standard constant-response regression-tree leaf predicts the ____ of its training responses, whereas a classification leaf usually predicts the most common class.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct answer:</strong> mean; arithmetic mean; average</p>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> Ordinary regression trees use the leaf response mean; classification trees use a majority or most frequent class. A tree with separate fitted regressions in leaves is a more elaborate specification, not the required default.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept to remember — Trees, predictions, branching, and pruning:</strong> Follow the feature tests to the reached leaf. A constant regression leaf predicts its mean, while a classification leaf uses its most common class. Check leaf support, training versus validation improvement, and pruning; impurity derivations are not required.</p>\n<p><strong>Further explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.statlearning.com/online-courses\">ISLR tree-model lectures</a> · <a href=\"https://statquest.org/video_index.html\">StatQuest video catalog: classification and regression trees</a></p>"}};
